*Sketch of the proof of Lemma <a href="#l.jitokeylem" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="l.jitokeylem">27</a>..* Assume that $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both $(\gamma(E) - \varepsilon ,
k)$-singular with $$d = m_2-m_1 > \frac{k+1}{2}.$$ We set $n_i = m_i - \left\lfloor \frac{3}{4} k \right\rfloor$, $i =
1,2$.

It may be shown that there is a polynomial $Q_k$ of degree $k$ such that $$\label{f.pkrep}
P_k(\tilde \omega) = Q_k\left( \cos \left( 2\pi (\tilde \omega + \frac{k-1}{2}
\alpha \right) \right).$$ Let $$\omega_j = \begin{cases} \omega + \left( n_1 + \frac{k-1}{2} + j
\right) \alpha  , & j = 0,1,\ldots, \left\lfloor \frac{k+1}{2}
\right\rfloor - 1, \\ \omega + \left( n_2 + \frac{k-1}{2} + j -
\left\lfloor \frac{k+1}{2} \right\rfloor \right) \alpha  , & j =
\left\lfloor \frac{k+1}{2} \right\rfloor , \left\lfloor
\frac{k+1}{2} \right\rfloor + 1,\ldots, k. \end{cases}$$ Lagrange interpolation then shows $$\label{f.jitoprooflagrange}
\left| Q_k(z) \right| = \left| \sum_{j=0}^k Q_k(\cos(2\pi\omega_j)
\frac{\prod_{l\not= j} (z - \cos (2\pi \omega_l)}{\prod_{l\not= j}
(\cos (2\pi \omega_j) - \cos (2\pi \omega_l))} \right|.$$ By $(\gamma(E) - \varepsilon ,
k)$-singularity and Lemma <a href="#l.jitolem0" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="l.jitolem0">25</a>, we have for $k$ sufficiently large, $$\label{f.jitoproofclaim1}
|Q_k(\cos(2\pi\omega_j) | < \exp \left( \frac{k}{8}(\gamma(E) -
\varepsilon) \right), \quad j = 0,1,\ldots,k.$$

Using that the Diophantine and non-resonance assumptions, one may show that if $d < k^\tau$ for some $\tau < 2$, we have for large $k$, $$\label{f.jitoproofclaim2}
\frac{|\prod_{l\not= j} (z - \cos (2\pi \omega_l)|}{|\prod_{l\not=
j} (\cos (2\pi \omega_j) - \cos (2\pi \omega_l))|} \le
\exp\left(\frac{k \varepsilon}{16} \right) \; \text{ for $z \in
[-1,1]$, } 0 \le j \le k.$$

