*<span class="smallcaps">**Proof**</span>.* **(Case $\natural=1$)** The explicit expression of the remainder symbol is $$\label{eeq32}
 \mathfrak{R}^1_\delta(p_\text{s},x_\text{s})=\delta^2\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}v_{n,m}\ \mbox{e}^{i2\pi (np_\text{s}+mx_\text{s})}\left[\mbox{e}^{i2\pi\delta I_{n,m}}-\left(\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}+i2\pi\delta I_{n,m}+\dfrac{1}{2}(i2\pi)^2\delta^2{I_{n,m}}^2\right)\right].$$ and from <a href="#eeq32" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq32">[eeq32]</a> it follows that $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta(p_\text{s},x_\text{s})\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{D},\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant\delta^2\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}|v_{n,m}|\Lambda_{n,m}$ with $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eeq33}
\Lambda_{n,m}:=\sup_{\psi\in\CMcal{H}_\text{f}} \setminus \{0\}
\dfrac{\left\|\left[\mbox{e}^{i2\pi\delta
I_{n,m}}-\left(\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}+i2\pi\delta
I_{n,m}+\dfrac{1}{2}(i2\pi)^2\delta^2{I_{n,m}}^2\right)\right]\Xi^{-1}\psi\right\|_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}}{\|\psi\|_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}}
\end{aligned}$$ since $\|\psi\|_{\CMcal{F}}:=\|\Xi\psi\|_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}$ and $\CMcal{F}=\Xi^{-1}\CMcal{H}_\text{f}$. The operators $I_{n,m}$ are essentially self-adjoint on $\CMcal{L}$ and we denote their closure with the same symbol. Since the operators ${I_{n,m}}^2$ are positive, we can consider the resolvent operators $R_{n,m}:=({I_{n,m}}^2+\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}})^{-1}$. Let suppose that $$\label{eeq34}
\zeta_{n,m}(\delta):= \left\|\left[\mbox{e}^{i2\pi\delta I_{n,m}}-\left(\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}+i2\pi\delta I_{n,m}+\dfrac{1}{2}(i2\pi)^2\delta^2{I_{n,m}}^2\right)\right]R_{n,m}\right\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant \zeta(\delta),$$ for all $n,m\in\mathbb Z$, with $\sup_\delta \zeta(\delta) < + \infty$. Then equation <a href="#eeq33" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq33">[eeq33]</a> would imply $$\Lambda_{n,m}\leqslant
\zeta(\delta)\left\|({I_{n,m}}^2+\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}})\,
\Xi^{-1}\right\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}.$$ Noticing that ${I_{n,m}}^2={\alpha_{n,m}}^2\ \mathfrak{a}^2+\overline{\alpha_{n,m}}^2\
{\mathfrak{a}^\dag}^2+2|\alpha_{n,m}|^2\ \Xi$ and observing that $\|\Xi^{-1}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}=2$, $\|{\mathfrak{a}}^2\Xi^{-1}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}=1$ and $\|{\mathfrak{a}^\dag}^2\Xi^{-1}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}=2\sqrt{2}$, one deduces from inequality <a href="#eeq34" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq34">[eeq34]</a> that $$\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta(p_\text{s},x_\text{s})\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{D},\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant C_1\left(\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}|v_{n,m}|(|n|+|m|)^2\right)\delta^2\zeta(\delta)\leqslant C_2 \delta^2\zeta(\delta)$$ for suitable positive constants $C_1$ and $C_2$.

It remains to prove the inequality <a href="#eeq34" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq34">[eeq34]</a> and the estimate on $\zeta(\delta)$. By spectral calculus one has that $\zeta_{n,m}(\delta)=\sup_{t\in\sigma(I_{n,m})}|Z_\delta(t)|\leqslant\sup_{t\in\mathbb R}|Z_\delta(t)|=:\zeta(\delta)$ where $$Z_\delta(t):=4\pi^2\delta^2\ \dfrac{\mbox{e}^{i2\pi\delta t}-\left(1+i2\pi\delta t-\dfrac{1}{2}(2\pi\delta t)^2\right)}{(2\pi\delta t)^2+4\pi^2\delta^2}.$$ After some manipulations and the change of variable $\tau:=2\pi\delta t$ one has that $$G_\delta(\tau):=\dfrac{1}{4\pi^4\delta^4}\left|Z_\delta\left(\dfrac{\tau}{2\pi\delta}\right)\right|^2\leqslant \dfrac{\tau^4+4\tau^2\cos(\tau)-8\tau\sin(\tau)-8\cos(\tau)+8}{\tau^4}<C_3.$$ Thus $\zeta(\delta)^2=4\pi^4\delta^4\
\sup_{\tau\in\mathbb R}G_\delta(\tau)\leqslant 4\pi^4C_3\delta^4$, hence $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta(p_\text{s},x_\text{s})\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{D},\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant
C\delta^4$. This concludes the first part of the proof.

Since $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\pi_\text{r}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant\sum_{i=1}^m\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta|\psi_{k_i}\rangle\langle\psi_{k_i}|\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}$, then it is enough to show that for any Hermite vector $\psi_k$ the inequality $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta|\psi_{k}\rangle\langle\psi_{k}|\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant
C_k\delta^5$ holds true. Observing that $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta|\psi_{k}\rangle\langle\psi_{k}|\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}=\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\psi_k\|_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}$, one deduces $$\begin{aligned}
 &\lim_{\delta\to0}\delta^{-5}\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta|\psi_{k}\rangle\langle\psi_{k}|\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}=\lim_{\delta\to0}\left\|\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}v_{n,m}\mbox{e}^{i2\pi (np_\text{s}+mx_\text{s})}\left(\sum_{j=3}^{+\infty}\dfrac{(i2\pi)^j\delta^{j-3}}{j!}{I_{n,m}}^j\right)\psi_k\right\|_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}\\
&\leqslant\dfrac{4}{3}\pi^3\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}|v_{n,m}|\ \|{I_{n,m}}^3\psi_k \|_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}\leqslant\dfrac{32}{3}\pi^3 C'  \|{A^\dag}^3\psi_k \|_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}=\dfrac{32}{3}\sqrt{(k+3)!}\ \pi^3 C'=:C_k
\end{aligned}$$ where $C':=\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}|v_{n,m}|\ |\alpha_{n,m}|^3$ is finite in view of Assumption ($\text{A}_\text{s}$).

This shows that for all $\delta\in[0,\delta_0)$ (for a suitable $\delta_0>0$) the norm $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta|\psi_{k}\rangle\langle\psi_{k}|\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}$ is bounded by $C_k\delta^5$ and so it follows that $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\pi_\text{r}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant
m C\delta^5$ with $C:=\max_{1,\ldots,m}\{C_{k_i}\}$. Finally $\|\pi_\text{r}\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}
=\|(\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\pi_\text{r})^\dag\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}
=\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\pi_\text{r}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}$.

This shows that for all $\delta\in[0,\delta_0)$ (for a suitable $\delta_0>0$) the norm $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta|\psi_{k}\rangle\langle\psi_{k}|\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}$ is bounded by $C_k\delta^5$ and so it follows that $\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\pi_\text{r}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant
m C\delta^5$ with $C:=\max_{1,\ldots,m}\{C_{k_i}\}$. Finally $\|\pi_\text{r}\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}
=\|(\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\pi_\text{r})^\dag\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}
=\|\mathfrak{R}^1_\delta\pi_\text{r}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}$.

**(Case $\natural=0$)** The proof proceeds as in the previous case. Divide the remainder symbol in two terms $\mathfrak{R}^0_\delta=\mathfrak{R}^0_0+ \mathfrak{R}^0_1$ where: $$\begin{aligned}
\mathfrak{R}^0_0(p_\text{s},x_\text{s})&:=\delta\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}\mbox{e}^{i2\pi (np_\text{s}+mx_\text{s})}\left(\mbox{e}^{i2\pi\delta I_{n,m}}-\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}-i2\pi\delta I_{n,m}\right)M^0_{n,m}\\
\mathfrak{R}^0_1(p_\text{s},x_\text{s})&:=\delta^2\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}v_{n,m}\ \mbox{e}^{i2\pi (np_\text{s}+mx_\text{s})}\left(\mbox{e}^{i2\pi\delta I_{n,m}}-\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}\right).
\end{aligned}$$ The control of $\mathfrak{R}^0_1$ is easy, indeed $\|\mathfrak{R}^0_1\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{D},\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant2\|\mathfrak{R}^0_1\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}\leqslant
4C\delta^2$ where $C:=\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}|v_{n,m}|$. Moreover (with the same technique used for the case $\natural=1$), one can check that for any Hermite vector $\psi_k$ the function $t_1(\delta):=\frac{1}{\delta^3}\|\mathfrak{R}^0_1(p_\text{s},x_\text{s})\psi_k\|_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}$ is bounded by a constant $C_k>0$ in a suitable interval $[0,\delta_0)$. This assures that $\|\mathfrak{R}^0_1\
\pi_\text{r}\|_{\mathscr{B}(\CMcal{H}_\text{f})}$ is of order $\CMcal{O}(\delta^3)$.

### Step 1. Almost-invariant subspace

As explained in the first part of proof of the Theorem <a href="#teo2" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="teo2">14</a> one constructs a formal symbol $\pi$ (the *Moyal projection*) such that: (i) $\pi\, \sharp \,\pi\asymp\pi$; (ii) $\pi^\dag=\pi$; (iii) $\widetilde{H}^\natural_\delta\, \sharp \,\pi\asymp\pi\, \sharp \,
\widetilde{H}^\natural_\delta$. Such a symbol $\pi\asymp\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\delta^j\pi_j$ is constructed recursively order by order starting from $\pi_0=\pi_\text{r}$ and $\widetilde{H}^\natural_\delta$ and it is unique (see Lemma 2.3. in ). The recursive relations are $$\label{eq_rec_p1}
\pi_n:=\pi^{\text{D}}_n+\pi^{\text{OD}}_n$$ where the *diagonal part* is $\pi^{\text{D}}_n:=\pi_\text{r}G_n\pi_\text{r}+(\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}-\pi_\text{r})G_n(\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}-\pi_\text{r})$ with $$\label{eq_rec_p2}
G_n:=\left[\left(\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\delta^j \pi_j\right)\sharp\left(\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\delta^j \pi_j\right)-\left(\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\delta^j \pi_j\right)\right]_n.$$ The *off-diagonal part* is defined by the implicit relation $[H_0;\pi^{\text{OD}}_n]=-F_n$ where $$\label{eq_rec_p3}
F_n:=\left[\widetilde{H}^\natural_\delta \, \sharp \,
\left(\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\delta^j \pi_j+\delta^n
\pi^{\text{D}}_n\right)-\left(\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\delta^j
\pi_j+\delta^n \pi^{\text{D}}_n\right)\, \sharp \,
\widetilde{H}^\natural_\delta\right]_n.$$

The quantization procedure can be reformulated by introducing the unitary operators $\mathcal{U}_{\infty}:=\mbox{e}^{-i2\pi Q_\text{s}}$ and $\mathcal{V}_{\infty}:=\mbox{e}^{-i2\pi P_\text{s}}$ (*Harper unitaries*), acting on $L^2(\mathds {R},dx)$ as (recall $\delta^2=\nicefrac{h_B}{2\pi}$) $$\label{eeq_har_unit}
(\mathcal{U}_{\infty}\psi)(x)=\mbox{e}^{- i 2\pi x}\psi(x), \qquad  \qquad
(\mathcal{V}_{\infty}\psi)(x)=\psi(x- \iota_qh_B) .$$

For any $\mathbb Z^2$-periodic function $F(p,x)=\sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}f_{n,m}\mbox{e}^{-i2\pi(np+mx)}$ whose Fourier series is uniformly convergent, the $h_B$-Weyl quantization of $F$ is given by the formula $$\label{eeq37}
 \widehat{F}(\mathcal{U}_{\infty},
\mathcal{V}_{\infty})= \sum_{n,m=-\infty}^{+\infty}f_{n,m}
\mbox{e}^{-i\pi nm(\iota_q h_B)}\ \mathcal{V}_{\infty}^n
\mathcal{U}_{\infty}^m.$$ where the fundamental commutation relation $\mathcal{U}_{\infty}
\mathcal{V}_{\infty}=\mbox{e}^{-i2\pi\left({\iota_q}{h_B}\right)}\
\mathcal{V}_{\infty} \mathcal{U}_{\infty}$ has been used. Formula <a href="#eeq37" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq37">[eeq37]</a> defines a *Harper-like Hamiltonian* with deformation parameter $h_B$. Indeed, the special case $H_\text{Har}=\CMcal{U}_{\infty}+\CMcal{U}_{\infty}^{-1}+\CMcal{V}_{\infty}+\CMcal{V}_{\infty}^{-1}$ is the celebrated Harper Hamiltonian, namely the operator acting on $L^2(\mathbb R,dx)$ as $$\label{eq54}
(H_\text{Har}\psi)(x)=\psi(x + h_B)+\psi(x - h_B) + 2\cos( 2\pi
x)\psi(x).$$

In analogy with Section 3, we summarize the discussion in the following conclusion

<div id="corol_har" class="concl">

## Coupling of Landau bands in a periodic magnetic potential

According to Theorem <a href="#teo1" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="teo1">23</a>, the first non-trivial term which describes the effective dynamics in the almost invariant subspace related to a single Landau band $\lambda_\ast$ is of order $\delta^2\propto h_B$. An important ingredient in the proof is that $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{A}}_\Gamma=0$ implies $H_1=0$. Moreover, the second non-trivial correction appears at order $\delta^4\propto{h_B}^2$ although $H_3\neq0$. Indeed, the correction at order $\delta^3$ vanishes since $H_3$, defined by <a href="#eeq29b" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq29b">[eeq29b]</a>, is linear in $\mathfrak{a}$ and $\mathfrak{a}^\dag$, hence $\langle \psi_\ast|H_3|\psi_\ast\rangle=0$. This observation suggests that for a family of Landau bands which contains two contiguous bands $\{ \lambda_{\ast},\lambda_{\ast+1}
\}$ one has, in general, a second non-trivial correction of order $\delta^3\propto{h_B}^{\frac{3}{2}}$ for the effective dynamics. Indeed, in this case one has $\pi_\text{r}H_3\pi_\text{r}\neq0$ since $\langle \psi_\ast|H_3|\psi_{\ast+1}\rangle$ is generally non zero. Nevertheless, also in this case, the first non-trivial correction is of order $\delta^2$.

Our goal is to derive the (non-trivial) leading order for the effective Hamiltonian in this framework. According to the notation of Theorem <a href="#teo1" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="teo1">23</a>, we need to expand the Moyal product $h^{\natural=0}= u\, \sharp \,\pi\, \sharp \,\widetilde{H}^0_\delta\, \sharp \,\pi\, \sharp \,
u^{\dag}=\pi_\text{r}\, \sharp \,u\, \sharp \,\widetilde{H}^0_\delta\, \sharp \,
u^{\dag}\, \sharp \,\pi_\text{r}+\CMcal{O}(\delta^\infty)$ up to the first order $\delta$. The symbols $\pi=\pi_\text{r}+\CMcal{O}(\delta)$ and $u=\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}+\CMcal{O}(\delta)$ are derived as in the general construction showed in the proof of Theorem <a href="#teo1" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="teo1">23</a>. Now $\CMcal{K}:=\text{Ran}\ \Pi_\text{r}\simeq
L^2(\mathbb R,dx_\text{s})\otimes\mathbb C^2$.

Expanding at zero order one finds $h_0= \pi_0 \, u_0 \, H_0
u_0^{-1} \,\pi_0= \pi_\text{r} \, \Xi \, \pi_\text{r} =\pi_\text{r}
\,\Xi = \Xi \, \pi_\text{r}$ and its quantization is the operator on $\CMcal{K}$ defined by $$\label{eeq42}
\text{Op}_\delta(h_0)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
 \left(n_\ast+\frac{3}{2}\right)\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{s}}&  0\\
 0& \left(n_\ast+\frac{1}{2}\right)\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{s}}
\end{array}\right)=(n_\ast+1)\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{K}}+\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{2}\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{s}}&  0\\
 0& -\frac{1}{2}\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{s}}
\end{array}\right)$$

Expanding at zero order one finds $h_0= \pi_0 \, u_0 \, H_0
u_0^{-1} \,\pi_0= \pi_\text{r} \, \Xi \, \pi_\text{r} =\pi_\text{r}
\,\Xi = \Xi \, \pi_\text{r}$ and its quantization is the operator on $\CMcal{K}$ defined by $$\label{eeq42}
\text{Op}_\delta(h_0)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
 \left(n_\ast+\frac{3}{2}\right)\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{s}}&  0\\
 0& \left(n_\ast+\frac{1}{2}\right)\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{s}}
\end{array}\right)=(n_\ast+1)\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{K}}+\left(\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{2}\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{s}}&  0\\
 0& -\frac{1}{2}\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{s}}
\end{array}\right)$$

As for the next order, from equation <a href="#eeq40" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq40">[eeq40]</a> it follows $\chi_1=H_1+u_1H_0-H_0u_1$ (we use $\chi_0=H_0$) which implies ${h}_1=\pi_\text{r}\chi_1\pi_\text{r}=\pi_\text{r} \,
H_1 \, \pi_\text{r}+\pi_\text{r}\,[u_1;\Xi]\,\pi_\text{r}$. To conclude the computation we need $u_1$ and $\pi_1$. Using the recursive formulas <a href="#eq_rec_p1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq_rec_p1">[eq_rec_p1]</a>, <a href="#eq_rec_p2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq_rec_p2">[eq_rec_p2]</a>, <a href="#eq_rec_p3" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq_rec_p3">[eq_rec_p3]</a>, <a href="#eq_rec_u1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq_rec_u1">[eq_rec_u1]</a>, <a href="#eq_rec_u2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq_rec_u2">[eq_rec_u2]</a> and <a href="#eq_rec_u3" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq_rec_u3">[eq_rec_u3]</a>, one obtains $-2a_{1}:=\left[u_0\, \sharp \,{u_0}^\dag-\mathds {1}_{\CMcal{H}_\text{f}}\right]_{1}=0$, $b_1:=[\pi_\text{r};B_1]$ and $B_1=[u_0\, \sharp \,\pi \, \sharp \,
{u_0}^\ast-\pi_\text{r}]_1=\pi_1$ since $a_1=0$. Observing that $G_1=[\pi_\text{r}\, \sharp \,\pi_\text{r}-\pi_\text{r}]_1=0$, it follows that $\pi^\text{D}_1=0$ and so $u_1=[\pi_\text{r};\pi_1]=[\pi_\text{r};\pi_1^\text{OD}]$ which implies $\pi_\text{r}u_1\pi_\text{r}=0$. Finally $\pi_\text{r}[u_1;\Xi]\pi_\text{r}=\pi_\text{r}[u_1;\pi_\text{r}\Xi\pi_\text{r}]\pi_\text{r}=0$ and so ${h}_1=\pi_\text{r}H_1\pi_\text{r}$. According to <a href="#eeq30a" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq30a">[eeq30a]</a> the quantization of ${h}_1$ is an operator which acts on $\CMcal{K}$ as $$\label{eeq43}
\text{Op}_\delta({h}_1)=\sqrt{n_\ast+1}\left(\begin{array}{cc}
0&  \ \mathcal{G}(P_\text{s},Q_\text{s})\\
\\
 \mathcal{G}(P_\text{s},Q_\text{s})^\dag& 0
\end{array}\right)$$ where the operator $\mathcal{G}(P_\text{s},Q_\text{s})$ is defined on $L^2(\mathbb R,dx_\text{s})$ as the Weyl quantization of the $\mathbb Z^2$-periodic function $g$ defined by equation <a href="#eeq31" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eeq31">[eeq31]</a>. Summarizing, we obtained the following result:

## The Landau Hamiltonian $H_\text{L}$

The *Landau Hamiltonian* is the operator $$\label{eq1appA1b}
H_\text{L}:=-\frac{1}{2}\Delta_M=\frac{1}{2}\left(K_1^2+K_2^2\right)=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(-i\frac{\partial}{\partial x_1}+\frac{1}{2}x_2\right)^2+\left(-i\frac{\partial}{\partial x_2}-\frac{1}{2}x_1\right)^2\right]$$ where $K_j:=-iD_j$, with $j=1,2$, are the *kinetic momenta*. The Landau Hamiltonian $H_\text{L}$ is essentially self-adjoint on on $C^\infty_\text{c}(\mathbb R^2;\mathbb C)\subset L^2(\mathbb R^2)$ (see Theorem X.34) and its domain of self-adjointness is exactly the second magnetic-Sobolev space $\mathcal{H}_{\text{\upshape M}}^2(\mathbb R^2)$ defined in Section <a href="#appA1" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="appA1">5.1</a>. To describe the spectrum of $H_\text{L}$ is helpful to introduce another pair of operators: $G_1:=-i\partial_{x_1}-\frac{1}{2}x_2$ and $G_2:=i\partial_{x_2}-\frac{1}{2}x_1$. The operators $K_1,K_2,G_1,G_2$ are all essentially self-adjoint on $C^\infty_\text{c}(\mathbb R^2;\mathbb C)$ (they have *deficiency indices* equal to zero) and on this domain the following commutation relations hold true $$\label{eq2appA1b}
[K_1;K_2]=[G_1;G_2]=i\mathds {1},\ \ \ [G_j;K_i]=0.$$ The last of the <a href="#eq1appA1b" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq1appA1b">[eq1appA1b]</a> implies $[G_j;H_\text{L}]=0$ hence the operators $G_1$ and $G_2$ are cause for the degeneration of the spectral eigenspaces of $H_\text{L}$. It is a common lore to introduce the *annihilation operator* $\mathfrak{a}:=\nicefrac{i}{\sqrt{2}}(K_2-iK_1)$ (its adjoint $\mathfrak{a}^\dag$ is called *creation operator*) and the *degeneration operator* $\mathfrak{g}:=\nicefrac{i}{\sqrt{2}}(G_2-iG_1)$. They fulfill the following (bosonic) commutation relation $$\label{eq3appA1b}
[\mathfrak{a};\mathfrak{a}^\dag]=[\mathfrak{g};\mathfrak{g}^\dag]=\mathds {1},\ \ \ [\mathfrak{g};H_{\text{L}}]=[\mathfrak{g}^\dag;H_{\text{L}}]=0,\ \ \ [\mathfrak{a};H_{\text{L}}]=\mathfrak{a},\ \ \ [\mathfrak{a}^\dag;H_{\text{L}}]=-\mathfrak{a}^\dag.$$ The last two relations follow from the equality $H_\text{L}=\mathfrak{a}\mathfrak{a}^\dag-\nicefrac{1}{2}\mathds {1}=\mathfrak{a}^\dag \mathfrak{a}+\nicefrac{1}{2}\mathds {1}$. Define the *ground state* $\psi_0\in L^2(\mathbb R^2)$ as the normalized solution of $\mathfrak{g}\psi_0=0=\mathfrak{a}\psi_0$, i.e. $\psi_0(x)=C\mbox{e}^{-\frac{1}{4}|x|^2}$. The *generalized Hermite function* of order $(n,m)$ is defined to be $\psi_{n,m}:=\frac{1}{\sqrt{n!m!}}\left(\mathfrak{g}^\dag\right)^m\left(\mathfrak{a}^\dag\right)^n\psi_0$. We will denote by $\CMcal{F}\subset L^2(\mathbb R^2)$ the set of the finite linear combinations of the vectors $\psi_{n,m}$ and we will call it the *Hermite domain*. Clearly $\CMcal{F}\subset S(\mathbb R^2)$ (the Schwartz space).

## The transform $\mathcal{W}$ built ‘‘by hand’’

Let $\CMcal{H}:=L^2(\mathbb R^2,d^2r)$ be the initial Hilbert space, with $r:=(r_1,r_2)$. Let $Q_r:=(Q_{r_1},Q_{r_2})$ where $Q_{r_j}$ is the multiplication operator by $r_j$ and $P_r:=(P_{r_1},P_{r_2})$ where $P_{r_1}:=-i\hslash\partial_{r_j}$, with $j=1,2$. Consider the *fast* and *slow* operators $$\label{eeqB1}
 (\text{fast})\ \left\{
\begin{aligned}
 &K_1:=-\frac{\alpha}{2\beta}\ v\cdot{Q}_r-\frac{\alpha\beta}{\hslash}\  w^\ast\cdot{P}_r\\
 &K_2:=\phantom{-}\frac{\alpha}{2\beta}\ w\cdot{Q}_r-\frac{\alpha\beta}{\hslash}\  v^\ast\cdot {P}_r
\end{aligned}
\right.\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \
(\text{slow})\ \left\{
\begin{aligned}
 &G_1:=\frac{1}{2}\ v\cdot {Q}_r-\frac{\beta^2}{\hslash}\ w^\ast\cdot {P}_r\\
&G_2:=\frac{1}{2}\ w\cdot {Q}_r+\frac{\beta^2}{\hslash}\ v^\ast\cdot {P}_r
\end{aligned}
\right.$$ with $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb C$ and $v,w,v^\ast,w^\ast\in\mathbb R^2$ such that $v\cdot v^\ast=w\cdot w^\ast=1$, $v^\ast\cdot w=v\cdot w^\ast=0$ and $|v\wedge w|=\ell^2>0$.

