The time evolution of an isolated macroscopic quantum system initially prepared in an out-of-equilibrium state is currently turning from an abstract concept to a real phenomenon that can be observed and studied experimentally. This striking change has been mainly driven by experiments on cold atoms, but it will be surely given further impulse in the near future by the fast progresses in time-resolved spectroscopy on condensed-matter systems.

We compute for $x =-\frac{n}{2},\dots,\frac{n}{2}-1$ (recall that ${\varphi}_{m}(\frac{n}{2}-1) = {\varphi}(-\frac{n}{2})$ due to our assumption of periodic boundary conditions) $$\begin{aligned}
