For normal diffusive spreading, $P(x,t)$ has a Gaussian form, $P(x,t)=e^{-\frac{x^2}{16Dt}}/{\sqrt{16\pi D t}}$, this $D$ being being the same as defined in Eq. <a href="#m_2nn" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="m_2nn">[m_2nn]</a>. In general, if $P(x,t)$ (and thereby $C(x,t)$) scales as $$\begin{aligned}
\label{Pxt_scaling}
P(x,t)\sim ({1}/{t^{\tilde{\beta}}})f({x}/{t^{\tilde{\beta}}}),
\end{aligned}$$ then $[m_{2n}(t)]^{1/n}\sim t^{2\tilde{\beta}}$. The connection to different regimes of transport as discussed before is immediate. However, there may occur cases where, $\tilde{\beta}=0.5$ but, $P(x,t)$ is not Gaussian. Such ‘non-Gaussian but diffusive’ transport has been reported in many classical systems . This is also considered as anomalous transport. Further, there may even occur cases where $P(x,t)$ does not follow a particular scaling form. As shown in Eq. <a href="#sigma_simpf" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sigma_simpf">[sigma_simpf]</a>, even then, the conductivity of the system depends only the time scaling of second moment, and classification of transport based on that is possible.

