Conversely, we consider another limit, the quenching process from a strong disorder strength $V_{i}\rightarrow\infty$ to the final $V_{f}=0$. By substituting Eqs. (<a href="#E12" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="E12">[E12]</a>),  (<a href="#E14" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="E14">[E14]</a>) and Eq. (<a href="#E19" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="E19">[E19]</a>) into Eq. (<a href="#E10" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="E10">[E10]</a>), we can get the return amplitude: $$\begin{aligned}
G_{n}(t) &=\braket{n|e^{-iH(V_{f})t}|n}\notag\\
&=\sum_{k}\braket{n|e^{-2it\sqrt{(J\cos{ka})^2+(\Delta \sin{ka})^2}}|k}\braket{k|n}\notag\\
&=\sum_{k}e^{-2it\sqrt{(J\cos{ka})^2+(\Delta \sin{ka})^2}}|\braket{k|n}|^2\notag\\
&=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k}e^{-2it\sqrt{(J\cos{ka})^2+(\Delta \sin{ka})^2}},
\end{aligned}$$ where $\ket{n}$ denotes $\ket{\Psi_{n}(V_{i}=\infty)}$. When $\Delta\ll J$ and in the large $N$ limit, the sum can be transformed into an integral. The same is true for $\Delta\gg J$ $$\begin{aligned}
G_{n}(t) & =\frac{a}{2\pi}\int_{-\frac{\pi}{a}}^{\frac{\pi}{a}}e^{-2iJt\cos ka}dk\notag\\
& =J_{0}(2Jt).
\label{second limit}
\end{aligned}$$

Therefore, the Loschmidt echo gets zero at times: $$t_{n}^{*}=\frac{x_{n}}{2J},$$ which are $1/2J$ of the zeros of the zero-order Bessel function $J_{0}(x)$, different to Eq. (<a href="#f_critical time" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="f_critical time">[f_critical time]</a>).

