With respect of the absence of particle interaction in Eq.<a href="#hs" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hs">[hs]</a>, it is convenient to focus our attention on the dynamics of single excitation. Under this prerequisite, the environment is set to be at zero temperature such that it can be at vacuum state. With respect of the environment at finite-temperature, the recent studies have shown that the localization may be destroyed by heating dynamics. As a result, the system relaxes eventually into a infinite-temperature state . However, the relaxation is logarithmically slow in time because of the localization in system . This means that there is a time window where the nonergodic feature of localization can be observed in experiments . So it is expected that the dynamics of localization can display some unusual feature at zero temperature because of the strong coupling between system and environment. Furthermore, the dynamics of localization can be treated exactly in this case. And thus one can get a comprehensive picture how the localization in the system is varied by coupling to environment. For this purpose, the dynamical equations of excitation must be derived first. Formally, at any time $t$, the state of the system plus environment can be written as $$\begin{aligned}
\label{state}
|\psi(t)\rangle&=& \left(\sum_{n=1}^N \alpha_n(t) |1\rangle_n|0\rangle^{\otimes (N-1)} \right)\otimes |0\rangle^{\otimes M} + \nonumber \\ &&|0\rangle^{\otimes N} \otimes \left(\sum_{k=1}^{M} \beta_k(t) |1\rangle_k|0\rangle^{\otimes (M-1)} \right),
\end{aligned}$$ where $|1\rangle_n = c_n^{\dagger}|0\rangle_n$ denotes the occupation of the $n$-th lattice site, $|0\rangle_k$ is the vacuum state of $b_k$ and $|1\rangle_k=b_k^{\dagger}|0\rangle_k$, and $M$ denotes the number of mode in the environment. Substituting Eq. <a href="#state" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="state">[state]</a> into Schrödinger equation and solving first for $\beta_k (t)$, one can get a integrodifferential equation for $\alpha_n(t)$, $$\begin{aligned}
\label{evolution}
\mathbbm{i}\frac{\partial }{\partial t}\alpha_n(t)&=& \left[\alpha_{n+1}(t) + \alpha_{n-1}(t)\right]+ \Delta \cos(2\pi \beta n +\phi)\alpha_n(t) \nonumber\\
&&- \mathbbm{i} \sum_{n=1}^N \int_0^t \text{d}\tau\alpha_n(\tau)f(t-\tau),
\end{aligned}$$ where $\mathbbm{i}$ is the square root of $-1$, and the memory kernel $f(t-\tau)$ is defined as $$\begin{aligned}
f(t-\tau)= \int_0^{\infty} \text{d} \omega J(\omega)e^{-\mathbbm{i} \omega(t-\tau)}.
\end{aligned}$$ It is evident that the population amplitude of $\alpha_n(t)$ is significantly correlated to its past values. For spectral density shown in Eq. <a href="#j" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="j">[j]</a>, $f(t-\tau)= \frac{\eta}{\omega_c^{s-1}} \frac{\Gamma(s+1)}{\left[\mathbbm{i}(t-\tau) + 1/\omega_c\right]^{s+1}}$. It is noted that the Markovian limit could be obtained by replacing $\alpha_n(\tau)$ in Eq. <a href="#evolution" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="evolution">[evolution]</a> with its current value $\alpha_n(t)$. As a result, the last term on the right hand of Eq. <a href="#state" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="state">[state]</a> contributes a positive term to Eq. <a href="#evolution" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="evolution">[evolution]</a>, which depicts the decaying of $\alpha_n(t)$ . So, it is expected that the non-Markovian feature would inspire the distinct dynamics of excitation.

# Coupling induced coherent transition among energy level $E$s

In general, the periodic oscillation of SP is a manifestation of the coherent transition between two orthogonal states. To verify this point, it is necessary to find the eigenstates by solving the Schrödinger equation as follows: $$\begin{aligned}
\label{h}
H|\psi_E\rangle= E |\psi_E\rangle,
\end{aligned}$$ in which $H=H_S + H_B +H_{int}$ is the total Hamiltonian of the system plus its environment. Formally, the eigenstate can be written as $|\psi_E\rangle= \left(\sum_{n=1}^N \alpha_n |1\rangle_n|0\rangle^{\otimes (N-1)} \right)\otimes |0\rangle^{\otimes M} +|0\rangle^{\otimes N} \otimes \left(\sum_{k=1}^{M} \beta_k |1\rangle_k|0\rangle^{\otimes (M-1)} \right)$. Substituting $|\psi_E\rangle$ into Eq. <a href="#h" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h">[h]</a> and eliminating $\beta_k$, we get $$\begin{aligned}
\label{psie}
\left(\alpha_{n+1} + \alpha_{n-1}\right) &+& \Delta \cos(2\pi \beta n +\phi)\alpha_n  +\nonumber \\
&& \int_0^{\infty} \text{d}\omega\frac{J(\omega)}{E-\omega} \sum_{n=1}^N \alpha_n = E \alpha_n.
\end{aligned}$$ By solving Eq.<a href="#psie" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="psie">[psie]</a>, the eigenenergy $E$ and the corresponding coefficient $\alpha_n$ can be determined, which characterizes the state of system after tracing out the environment. In this sense, we call the state $\sum_n \alpha_n |1\rangle_n$ as the reduced energy eigenstate of system. As shown in Appendix, because of the integral $\int_0^{\infty} \text{d}\omega\frac{J(\omega)}{E-\omega}$, $E$ can be complex, and thus Eq.<a href="#psie" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="psie">[psie]</a> depicts the nonunitary dynamics of single excitation in the atomic chain. Formally, one can introduce an effective non-Hermitian Hamiltonian to characterize the nonunitary dynamics. In this point, the state $\sum_n \alpha |1\rangle_n$ can be considered as the eigenstate of the non-Hermitian Hamiltonian. However, it is difficult to construct the counterpart in this case since $E$ is involved in Eq. <a href="#psie" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="psie">[psie]</a>. Thus, in order to find $E$ and corresponding $\alpha_n$s, one has to resort to numerical method.

In general, the periodic oscillation of SP is a manifestation of the coherent transition between two orthogonal states. To verify this point, it is necessary to find the eigenstates by solving the Schrödinger equation as follows: $$\begin{aligned}
\label{h}
H|\psi_E\rangle= E |\psi_E\rangle,
\end{aligned}$$ in which $H=H_S + H_B +H_{int}$ is the total Hamiltonian of the system plus its environment. Formally, the eigenstate can be written as $|\psi_E\rangle= \left(\sum_{n=1}^N \alpha_n |1\rangle_n|0\rangle^{\otimes (N-1)} \right)\otimes |0\rangle^{\otimes M} +|0\rangle^{\otimes N} \otimes \left(\sum_{k=1}^{M} \beta_k |1\rangle_k|0\rangle^{\otimes (M-1)} \right)$. Substituting $|\psi_E\rangle$ into Eq. <a href="#h" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h">[h]</a> and eliminating $\beta_k$, we get $$\begin{aligned}
\label{psie}
\left(\alpha_{n+1} + \alpha_{n-1}\right) &+& \Delta \cos(2\pi \beta n +\phi)\alpha_n  +\nonumber \\
&& \int_0^{\infty} \text{d}\omega\frac{J(\omega)}{E-\omega} \sum_{n=1}^N \alpha_n = E \alpha_n.
\end{aligned}$$ By solving Eq.<a href="#psie" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="psie">[psie]</a>, the eigenenergy $E$ and the corresponding coefficient $\alpha_n$ can be determined, which characterizes the state of system after tracing out the environment. In this sense, we call the state $\sum_n \alpha_n |1\rangle_n$ as the reduced energy eigenstate of system. As shown in Appendix, because of the integral $\int_0^{\infty} \text{d}\omega\frac{J(\omega)}{E-\omega}$, $E$ can be complex, and thus Eq.<a href="#psie" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="psie">[psie]</a> depicts the nonunitary dynamics of single excitation in the atomic chain. Formally, one can introduce an effective non-Hermitian Hamiltonian to characterize the nonunitary dynamics. In this point, the state $\sum_n \alpha |1\rangle_n$ can be considered as the eigenstate of the non-Hermitian Hamiltonian. However, it is difficult to construct the counterpart in this case since $E$ is involved in Eq. <a href="#psie" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="psie">[psie]</a>. Thus, in order to find $E$ and corresponding $\alpha_n$s, one has to resort to numerical method.

In Appendix, Eq. <a href="#psie" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="psie">[psie]</a> is solved numerically for $a=0, \Delta=2.5$ and $a=0.5, \Delta=1$ respectively. Focusing on two highest $E$, we found that the difference of their real parts is consistent with the oscillation frequency of SR, observed in previous section. For instance, it is found for $a=0$ and $\Delta=2.5$ that the oscillation of SR has a frequency of $\sim 2\pi/77.8=0.08076$. Correspondingly, the calculated difference between the real parts of two highest $E$s is $2.952238 - 2.882305=0.069933$, as shown in Fig. <a href="#fig:eta" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="fig:eta">[fig:eta]</a>(a). Similar observation can also be found for $a=0.5$ and $\Delta=1$, that SR shows an oscillation with frequency $\sim 2\pi/57.6=0.109083$. The calculated difference is $2.705113- 2.605926=0.099187$, as shown in Fig. <a href="#fig:eta" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="fig:eta">[fig:eta]</a>(b). The small discrepancy can be attributed to the error of estimation. Moreover, it is noted that the imaginary part of the highest $E$ has a order of magnitude $\sim 10^{-6}$ in both cases. This means that the decaying of SP and IPR is slow and neither display a discernable descent in numerical simulation up to $t\sim 10^3$. In further, we also calculate the overlap between the initial state of system and the state $|\psi_{ES}\rangle$ corresponding to the highest $E$. For $a=0, \Delta=2.5$, $\left|\langle ES | \psi_{ES}\rangle\right|^2=0.498776$, which is consistent to the extremal maximum of SP $0.496809$ extracted from the data in Fig. <a href="#fig:a=0" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="fig:a=0">[fig:a=0]</a>. While for $a=0.5, \Delta=1$, $\left|\langle ES | \psi_{ES}\rangle\right|^2=0.4471716$, which is consistent to the extremal maximum of SP 0.405453 extracted from the data in Fig. <a href="#fig:a=0.5" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="fig:a=0.5">[fig:a=0.5]</a>.

