# Measurement of effective coupling strengths

The system can be fully described by the generalized Aubry-André-Harper (GAAH) model with the Hamiltonian: $$\begin{aligned}
    &\frac{\hat H }{\hbar} = \sum\limits_{j=1}^{9} ({ J_{j, j+1}{\hat a}_j^{\dagger} {{\hat a}_{j + 1}}+ \text{H.c.}})+\sum\limits_{j=1}^{10} { h_j{\hat a}_j^{\dagger} {{\hat a}_{j}}}.
    \label{eq_SGAAH}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $J_{j, j+1} = \lambda \left( 1 + \mu \cos \left[ {2\pi \left( {j + \frac{1}{2}} \right)\alpha  + \delta } \right]\right)$ and $h_j = \lambda V\cos (2\pi j\alpha  + \delta )$ represent the effective coupling of qubits and the frequency detuning relative to resonant frequency ($\simeq4.36$ GHz). In this paper, we set $\lambda/2\pi=4$ MHz and $\alpha = \left(\sqrt{5}-1\right)/2$. $\mu$ and $V$ represent modulations amplitudes of off-diagonal hopping and on-site incommensurate potential, respectively. We manipulate $\mu$ and $V$ by applying the fast voltages to the corresponding Z control lines of the coupler and qubit respectively.

In our processor of coupler architecture, the effective coupling of qubits can be adjusted by tuning couplers frequencies. Mathematically, the effective coupling strength can be described by :

