We consider a linear transformation $\bm\Psi'=V\bm\Psi$, where $V=\openone_2\otimes{}u+\sigma_x\otimes{}v$ is orthogonal, with $u$, $v$ being real matrices. The transformation is canonical due to the preservation of the anticommutation relations: $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:can_cond}
&&uu^T+vv^T=\openone\, \quad {\rm and} \quad  uv^T+(uv^T)^T=0\ ,
\end{aligned}$$ Under the real canonical transformations, the Hamiltonian becomes $H=({\bm\Psi'}^\dagger{}C'\bm\Psi'+\mathrm{Tr}A)/2$, where the eigenvalue matrix $C'=VCV^T=\sigma_z\otimes\Lambda$ with the $N\times{}N$ positive semi-definite diagonal matrix $\Lambda=\mathrm{diag}(\Lambda_1,\dots,\Lambda_N)$. Here the fact that the eigenvalues of $C$ appear in pairs of real numbers of opposite sign is guaranteed by the imposed particle-hole symmetry in the Nambu representation.

In fact, all information of Eq. (<a href="#eq:Hamfree" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:Hamfree">[eq:Hamfree]</a>), including ground-state properties, can be decoded from a $N^2$-dimensional auxiliary real matrix $Z \equiv A - B$ . One can thus find $Z'=A'-B'$ satisfies the simple relation $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:Z'}
Z'=(u+v)Z(u-v)^T \ .
\end{aligned}$$ One then has $C'=\sigma_z\otimes{}A'+i\sigma_y\otimes{}B'$, so that $A'=\Lambda$, $B'=0$ and hence $Z'=A'-B'=\Lambda$. From Eq. (<a href="#eq:Z&#39;" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:Z&#39;">[eq:Z']</a>), by defining $\Phi\equiv{}u+v$ and $\Psi\equiv{}u-v$, one finally gets the important equation $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:PhiZPsi^T}
\Phi{}Z\Psi^T = \Lambda  \quad {\rm and} \quad \Psi{}Z^T\Phi^T=\Lambda \ .
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently, $\Phi$, $\Psi$, and $\Lambda$ are simply given by the *singular value decomposition* of $Z=\Phi^{T}\Lambda\Psi$. Due to the canonical conditions, the matrices $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ must be orthogonal. Then we can derive straightforwardly the following relations $\Lambda\Psi=\Phi{}Z$, $\Lambda\Phi=\Psi{}Z^T$. As a consequence, $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ can be calculated by diagonalizing $ZZ^T$ or $Z^TZ$, given by $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:ZZT}
\Phi{}ZZ^T=\Lambda^2\Phi, \quad {\rm and} \quad
\Psi{}Z^TZ=\Lambda^2\Psi.
\end{aligned}$$ Note that $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ cannot be calculated by diagonalizing $ZZ^T$ and $Z^TZ$ independently because of Eq. (<a href="#eq:PhiZPsi^T" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:PhiZPsi^T">[eq:PhiZPsi^T]</a>). After solving Eq. (<a href="#eq:ZZT" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:ZZT">[eq:ZZT]</a>) for orthogonal matrices $\Phi$, $\Psi$ and diagonal matrix $\Lambda$, one introduces $u\equiv(\Phi+\Psi)/2$ and $v\equiv(\Phi-\Psi)/2$, in terms of which the canonically transformed operators diagonalizing the Hamiltonian are defined by $$\begin{aligned}
\eta_k\equiv\sum_{j=1}^N(u_{k,j}^{}c_j^{}+v_{k,j}^{}c_j^\dagger).
\end{aligned}$$ Finally, the Hamiltonian reads $$\begin{aligned}
H = \sum_{k=1}^N\Lambda_k^{}\eta_k^\dagger\eta_k^{}+E_0 \ ,
\end{aligned}$$ where $E_0=\mathrm{Tr}(A-\Lambda)/2$ is the ground-state energy with $\Lambda_k$ being single particle energies.

In fact, all information of Eq. (<a href="#eq:Hamfree" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:Hamfree">[eq:Hamfree]</a>), including ground-state properties, can be decoded from a $N^2$-dimensional auxiliary real matrix $Z \equiv A - B$ . One can thus find $Z'=A'-B'$ satisfies the simple relation $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:Z'}
Z'=(u+v)Z(u-v)^T \ .
\end{aligned}$$ One then has $C'=\sigma_z\otimes{}A'+i\sigma_y\otimes{}B'$, so that $A'=\Lambda$, $B'=0$ and hence $Z'=A'-B'=\Lambda$. From Eq. (<a href="#eq:Z&#39;" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:Z&#39;">[eq:Z']</a>), by defining $\Phi\equiv{}u+v$ and $\Psi\equiv{}u-v$, one finally gets the important equation $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:PhiZPsi^T}
\Phi{}Z\Psi^T = \Lambda  \quad {\rm and} \quad \Psi{}Z^T\Phi^T=\Lambda \ .
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently, $\Phi$, $\Psi$, and $\Lambda$ are simply given by the *singular value decomposition* of $Z=\Phi^{T}\Lambda\Psi$. Due to the canonical conditions, the matrices $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ must be orthogonal. Then we can derive straightforwardly the following relations $\Lambda\Psi=\Phi{}Z$, $\Lambda\Phi=\Psi{}Z^T$. As a consequence, $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ can be calculated by diagonalizing $ZZ^T$ or $Z^TZ$, given by $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:ZZT}
\Phi{}ZZ^T=\Lambda^2\Phi, \quad {\rm and} \quad
\Psi{}Z^TZ=\Lambda^2\Psi.
\end{aligned}$$ Note that $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ cannot be calculated by diagonalizing $ZZ^T$ and $Z^TZ$ independently because of Eq. (<a href="#eq:PhiZPsi^T" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:PhiZPsi^T">[eq:PhiZPsi^T]</a>). After solving Eq. (<a href="#eq:ZZT" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:ZZT">[eq:ZZT]</a>) for orthogonal matrices $\Phi$, $\Psi$ and diagonal matrix $\Lambda$, one introduces $u\equiv(\Phi+\Psi)/2$ and $v\equiv(\Phi-\Psi)/2$, in terms of which the canonically transformed operators diagonalizing the Hamiltonian are defined by $$\begin{aligned}
\eta_k\equiv\sum_{j=1}^N(u_{k,j}^{}c_j^{}+v_{k,j}^{}c_j^\dagger).
\end{aligned}$$ Finally, the Hamiltonian reads $$\begin{aligned}
H = \sum_{k=1}^N\Lambda_k^{}\eta_k^\dagger\eta_k^{}+E_0 \ ,
\end{aligned}$$ where $E_0=\mathrm{Tr}(A-\Lambda)/2$ is the ground-state energy with $\Lambda_k$ being single particle energies.

The ground state can be obtained by imposing the condition $\eta_k |\Psi_0\rangle=0,\; \forall \; k$ . Recalling the *singular value decomposition* of $Z$, $$\begin{aligned}
Z=\Phi^{T}\Lambda\Psi=(\Phi^{T}\Lambda \Phi^{*})(\Phi^{T}\Psi)  = P T,
\end{aligned}$$ giving rise to the polar decomposition of $Z$ such that $Z=P T$, where $P \equiv  \sqrt{ZZ^\dagger} = \Phi^{T}\Lambda \Phi^{*}$ is a positive semi-definite matrix and $T=\Phi^{T}\Psi$ is unitary. The ground state has an explicit BCS-like form in the case of even parity $$\begin{aligned}
\label{Eq:Psi0}
\vert g_{Z} \rangle= \mathcal{N} \exp{\left( \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j,k=1}^{N} c_j^{\dagger} G_{jk} c_k^{\dagger} \right)}
\vert 0 \rangle,
\end{aligned}$$ where $\mathcal{N}$ is a normalization factor, $|0\rangle$ is the fermionic vacuum ($c_j|0\rangle=0$) and $G$ is a real $N\times N$ antisymmetric matrix, which satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:gG+h}
uG+v=0 \ .
\end{aligned}$$ Note that Eq.(<a href="#eq:gG+h" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:gG+h">[eq:gG+h]</a>) is not always solvable, corresponding to cases where either the ansatz (<a href="#Eq:Psi0" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="Eq:Psi0">[Eq:Psi0]</a>) does not hold or the ground state parity is odd. We will put these exceptions on hold for the moment. We will assume $Z$ is invertible and $T$ is well defined in the following.

The ground state can be obtained by imposing the condition $\eta_k |\Psi_0\rangle=0,\; \forall \; k$ . Recalling the *singular value decomposition* of $Z$, $$\begin{aligned}
Z=\Phi^{T}\Lambda\Psi=(\Phi^{T}\Lambda \Phi^{*})(\Phi^{T}\Psi)  = P T,
\end{aligned}$$ giving rise to the polar decomposition of $Z$ such that $Z=P T$, where $P \equiv  \sqrt{ZZ^\dagger} = \Phi^{T}\Lambda \Phi^{*}$ is a positive semi-definite matrix and $T=\Phi^{T}\Psi$ is unitary. The ground state has an explicit BCS-like form in the case of even parity $$\begin{aligned}
\label{Eq:Psi0}
\vert g_{Z} \rangle= \mathcal{N} \exp{\left( \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j,k=1}^{N} c_j^{\dagger} G_{jk} c_k^{\dagger} \right)}
\vert 0 \rangle,
\end{aligned}$$ where $\mathcal{N}$ is a normalization factor, $|0\rangle$ is the fermionic vacuum ($c_j|0\rangle=0$) and $G$ is a real $N\times N$ antisymmetric matrix, which satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:gG+h}
uG+v=0 \ .
\end{aligned}$$ Note that Eq.(<a href="#eq:gG+h" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:gG+h">[eq:gG+h]</a>) is not always solvable, corresponding to cases where either the ansatz (<a href="#Eq:Psi0" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="Eq:Psi0">[Eq:Psi0]</a>) does not hold or the ground state parity is odd. We will put these exceptions on hold for the moment. We will assume $Z$ is invertible and $T$ is well defined in the following.

When $P$ (and hence $Z$) is invertible, by using Eq. (<a href="#eq:PhiZPsi^T" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:PhiZPsi^T">[eq:PhiZPsi^T]</a>) one can write $u=\Phi(\openone+P_\Phi^{-1}Z)/2$, $v=\Phi(\openone-P_\Phi^{-1}Z)/2$, with $P_\Phi \equiv \Phi^{-1}P\Phi$, so that if $u$ is invertible one has $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:G}
G = \frac{T-\openone}{T+\openone} \ ,
\end{aligned}$$ where $G$ is the Cayley transform of $T\equiv P_\Phi^{-1}Z$, which is the orthogonal part of the polar decomposition of $Z$. From Eq. (<a href="#eq:G" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:G">[eq:G]</a>), the orthogonality $T^T=T^{-1}$ readily implies the antisymmetry $G^T=-G$. The inverse Cayley transform then yields $T=(\openone+G)/(\openone-G)$, implying $\det{T}=1$. We can then find that the spectrum of the real antisymmetric matrix $G$ is given by complex conjugate pairs of purely imaginary eigenvalues.

When $P$ (and hence $Z$) is invertible, by using Eq. (<a href="#eq:PhiZPsi^T" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:PhiZPsi^T">[eq:PhiZPsi^T]</a>) one can write $u=\Phi(\openone+P_\Phi^{-1}Z)/2$, $v=\Phi(\openone-P_\Phi^{-1}Z)/2$, with $P_\Phi \equiv \Phi^{-1}P\Phi$, so that if $u$ is invertible one has $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:G}
G = \frac{T-\openone}{T+\openone} \ ,
\end{aligned}$$ where $G$ is the Cayley transform of $T\equiv P_\Phi^{-1}Z$, which is the orthogonal part of the polar decomposition of $Z$. From Eq. (<a href="#eq:G" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:G">[eq:G]</a>), the orthogonality $T^T=T^{-1}$ readily implies the antisymmetry $G^T=-G$. The inverse Cayley transform then yields $T=(\openone+G)/(\openone-G)$, implying $\det{T}=1$. We can then find that the spectrum of the real antisymmetric matrix $G$ is given by complex conjugate pairs of purely imaginary eigenvalues.

With the above wisdom, it is ready to calculate the ground-state fidelity as $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:fid0}
&&F(Z,\tilde{Z})  \equiv  \vert\langle g_Z \vert g_{\tilde{Z}}\rangle\vert.
\end{aligned}$$ We give an explicit evaluation of the fidelity (<a href="#eq:fid0" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:fid0">[eq:fid0]</a>) from the unitary matrix $T$, which can be further simplified into the following form by recognizing that (<a href="#Eq:Psi0" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="Eq:Psi0">[Eq:Psi0]</a>) is a fermionic coherent state : $$\begin{aligned}
 \label{eq:fid}
F(Z,\tilde{Z})&=&\frac{ \det(\openone + G^\dagger \tilde G)^{1/2} }
{\det(\openone + G^\dagger G)^{1/4}\det(\openone + \tilde{G}^\dagger \tilde{G})^{1/4}}\nonumber \\
&=&\sqrt{\vert \det{\frac{\openone+T^{-1}\tilde{T}}{2}} \vert}=\sqrt{\vert \det{\frac{T+\tilde{T}}{2}} \vert}, \quad
\end{aligned}$$ where $T$ and $\tilde{T}$ are respectively the unitary matrices of $Z \equiv Z(V)$ and $\tilde{Z} \equiv Z(V+\delta V)$ for infinitesimally close parameters $V$ and $V+\delta V$.

With the above wisdom, it is ready to calculate the ground-state fidelity as $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:fid0}
&&F(Z,\tilde{Z})  \equiv  \vert\langle g_Z \vert g_{\tilde{Z}}\rangle\vert.
\end{aligned}$$ We give an explicit evaluation of the fidelity (<a href="#eq:fid0" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:fid0">[eq:fid0]</a>) from the unitary matrix $T$, which can be further simplified into the following form by recognizing that (<a href="#Eq:Psi0" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="Eq:Psi0">[Eq:Psi0]</a>) is a fermionic coherent state : $$\begin{aligned}
 \label{eq:fid}
F(Z,\tilde{Z})&=&\frac{ \det(\openone + G^\dagger \tilde G)^{1/2} }
{\det(\openone + G^\dagger G)^{1/4}\det(\openone + \tilde{G}^\dagger \tilde{G})^{1/4}}\nonumber \\
&=&\sqrt{\vert \det{\frac{\openone+T^{-1}\tilde{T}}{2}} \vert}=\sqrt{\vert \det{\frac{T+\tilde{T}}{2}} \vert}, \quad
\end{aligned}$$ where $T$ and $\tilde{T}$ are respectively the unitary matrices of $Z \equiv Z(V)$ and $\tilde{Z} \equiv Z(V+\delta V)$ for infinitesimally close parameters $V$ and $V+\delta V$.

Using $\sqrt{\det(M)}=\det(\sqrt{M})$ and $\det(e^M)=e^{\textrm{Tr}(M)}$, Eq.(<a href="#eq:fid" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:fid">[eq:fid]</a>) can be further simplified as $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:derivation_1}
\lefteqn{F(Z,\tilde{Z})=
\sqrt{\vert \det{\frac{T+\tilde{T}}{2}} \vert}}\nonumber \\ &=&
\exp\left\{\textrm{Tr} \ln{\left(\frac{1+T^{\dagger}\tilde{T}}{2}\right)^{1/2}}\right\}\nonumber \\
&=&\exp\left\{\textrm{Tr} \ln{\left(\frac{1+T^{\dagger}\left(T+\delta T \right) }{2}\right)^{1/2}}\right\}\nonumber \\
&=&\exp\left\{\textrm{Tr} \ln{\left(1+\frac{T^{\dagger}\delta T}{2}\right)^{1/2}}\right\}\nonumber \\
&=&\exp\left\{\textrm{Tr} \frac{1}{2}\ln{\left(1+\frac{T^{\dagger}\delta T}{2}\right)}\right\}\nonumber \\
&\approx&\exp\left\{\textrm{Tr}   \left[ \frac{1}{4}(T^{\dagger}\delta T)-\frac{1}{16}\left( T^{\dagger}\delta T \right)^2 \right]\right\},
\end{aligned}$$ where $\delta T=\partial_{V}T d V$. The fidelity susceptibility is thus obtained by $$\label{eq:chi}
\chi_{\rm F} (V)=\lim_{\delta V \rightarrow 0} \frac{-2 \ln{F}}{\delta V^2}.$$ To this end, Eq.(<a href="#eq:chi" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="eq:chi">[eq:chi]</a>) can be rewritten in terms of the unitary matrix $T$ as $$\label{eq:chifrob}
\chi_{\rm F} =\frac{1}{8}\Vert\partial_{V}T \Vert_{F}^{2},$$ where $\Vert M \Vert_{F}$$\equiv \sqrt{\textrm{Tr}(M M^{\dagger})}$ is the Frobenius norm. As is observed in Fig.<a href="#fig:GSFS" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="fig:GSFS">[fig:GSFS]</a>, the fidelity susceptibility manifests divergent peaks at $V_{c2}$, while there is no anomaly across $V_{c1}$ without gap closing.

