# Intermediate localization regime in the Aubry-André chain

The AA Hamiltonian with an incommensurate potential on the 1D chain reads  $$\begin{aligned}
  \mathcal{H}= \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} [-t(\ketbra{n}{n+1} + \ketbra{n}{n-1}) + \lambda \cos(\alpha n + \phi) \ketbra{n}{n} ]\;.
  \label{eq:full_aa_ham}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $t \in \mathbb{R}$ is the n.n. hopping parameter, while $\alpha/2\pi$ is an irrational number ensuring the quasiperiodicity of the potential. Without losing generality we consider $\alpha > 0$. The potential $\cos(\alpha n + \phi)$ almost repeats itself at lattice sites $n$ and $n + \lfloor2\pi/\alpha\rfloor$ (or $n + \lceil2\pi/\alpha\rceil$) for arbitrary $n$. Therefore we define the *winding length* $$\begin{aligned}
  \mathcal{W}= \lceil 2\pi/\alpha \rceil \geq 1\;.                                                                                                                                                                                                                            
\end{aligned}$$ The Hamiltonian <a href="#eq:full_aa_ham" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:full_aa_ham">[eq:full_aa_ham]</a> has *asymptotically* exponentially localized eigenstates for $\abs{\lambda} > 2\abs{t}$, which are peaked around some eigenstate dependent lattice site $n_0$. The asymptotic localization length $$\begin{aligned}
  \label{eq:ll}
  \xi = \frac{1}{\ln\abs{\frac{\lambda}{2t}}}
\end{aligned}$$ describes the asymptotic decay of all eigenstates $\psi_n \sim {\rm e}^{-|n|/\xi}$ and is independent of the winding length $\mathcal{W}$ and the phase $\phi$ .

## Large winding length

We now demonstrate that there is an *intermediate localization* regime whose span is governed by the winding length $\mathcal{W}$ and the phase $\phi$. In this intermediate regime eigenfunctions decay faster than exponential similarly to the eigenstates of the Wannier-Stark ladder. For that we consider the case of very small values of $\abs{\alpha} \ll 1$: there is a region of the 1D chain, a subset of the chain sites $\mathbb{L} \subset \mathbb{Z}$, such that for all $n \in \mathbb{L}$ and a particular eigenstate peak $n_0 \in \mathbb{L}$ the following condition holds $$\begin{aligned}
  \abs{\alpha (n - n_0)}\ll 1\;.
\end{aligned}$$ Therefore we can approximate the cosine potential up to the second order expansion in $\alpha (n - n_0)$ as $$\begin{aligned}
  \cos(\alpha n + \phi) & \approx  
  \cos(\alpha n_0 + \phi) \left[1 - \frac{\alpha^2}{2}{(n - n_0)}^2 \right] \notag \\
  & -\sin(\alpha n_0 + \phi) \left[ \alpha(n-n_0)\right]\;. 
  \label{eq:aa_pot_term}
\end{aligned}$$ The choice of the phase $\phi$ allows to approximate either linear or quadratic potentials. For $\alpha n_0 + \phi \approx (2m+1)\pi/2$ with $m\in \mathbb{Z}$, the Hamiltonian <a href="#eq:full_aa_ham" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:full_aa_ham">[eq:full_aa_ham]</a> is approximated by the Wannier-Stark Hamiltonian (WS) on a 1D chain $$\begin{aligned}
  \mathcal{H}= -t\sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}}  \ketbra{n}{n+1} + \ketbra{n}{n-1} \notag \\
  -{(-1)}^m \lambda \alpha \sum_{n\in \mathbb{Z}} (n - n_0) \ketbra{n}{n}\;. 
  \label{eq:sin_poly1}
\end{aligned}$$ The effective DC field strength is given by $\mathcal{F}=\lambda\alpha$ which has to be compared to the hopping strength $t$ . Given $\abs{\alpha}\ll 1$, one needs strong potential strengths $\abs{\lambda/t}\gg 1$ to achieve even moderate field values $\mathcal{F}\approx t$.

We now demonstrate that there is an *intermediate localization* regime whose span is governed by the winding length $\mathcal{W}$ and the phase $\phi$. In this intermediate regime eigenfunctions decay faster than exponential similarly to the eigenstates of the Wannier-Stark ladder. For that we consider the case of very small values of $\abs{\alpha} \ll 1$: there is a region of the 1D chain, a subset of the chain sites $\mathbb{L} \subset \mathbb{Z}$, such that for all $n \in \mathbb{L}$ and a particular eigenstate peak $n_0 \in \mathbb{L}$ the following condition holds $$\begin{aligned}
  \abs{\alpha (n - n_0)}\ll 1\;.
\end{aligned}$$ Therefore we can approximate the cosine potential up to the second order expansion in $\alpha (n - n_0)$ as $$\begin{aligned}
  \cos(\alpha n + \phi) & \approx  
  \cos(\alpha n_0 + \phi) \left[1 - \frac{\alpha^2}{2}{(n - n_0)}^2 \right] \notag \\
  & -\sin(\alpha n_0 + \phi) \left[ \alpha(n-n_0)\right]\;. 
  \label{eq:aa_pot_term}
\end{aligned}$$ The choice of the phase $\phi$ allows to approximate either linear or quadratic potentials. For $\alpha n_0 + \phi \approx (2m+1)\pi/2$ with $m\in \mathbb{Z}$, the Hamiltonian <a href="#eq:full_aa_ham" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:full_aa_ham">[eq:full_aa_ham]</a> is approximated by the Wannier-Stark Hamiltonian (WS) on a 1D chain $$\begin{aligned}
  \mathcal{H}= -t\sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}}  \ketbra{n}{n+1} + \ketbra{n}{n-1} \notag \\
  -{(-1)}^m \lambda \alpha \sum_{n\in \mathbb{Z}} (n - n_0) \ketbra{n}{n}\;. 
  \label{eq:sin_poly1}
\end{aligned}$$ The effective DC field strength is given by $\mathcal{F}=\lambda\alpha$ which has to be compared to the hopping strength $t$ . Given $\abs{\alpha}\ll 1$, one needs strong potential strengths $\abs{\lambda/t}\gg 1$ to achieve even moderate field values $\mathcal{F}\approx t$.

For $\alpha n_0 + \phi \approx m\pi$ with $m\in \mathbb{Z}$ the AA Hamiltonian <a href="#eq:full_aa_ham" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:full_aa_ham">[eq:full_aa_ham]</a> approximates the discrete simple harmonic oscillator Hamiltonian (SHO) on a 1D chain $$\begin{aligned}
  \mathcal{H}& = - t  \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} \ketbra{n}{n+1} + \ketbra{n}{n-1} \notag \\
  & + {(-1)}^m \lambda \sum_{n\in \mathbb{Z}}\left[1 - \frac{\alpha^2}{2}{(n - n_0)}^2 \right] \ketbra{n}{n}\;. 
  \label{eq:cos_poly2}
\end{aligned}$$ The harmonic potential strength is given by $\mathcal{F}= \lambda\alpha^2/2$.

## Asymptotic decay of eigenfunctions for power-law potentials on a chain

We start by discussing the localization properties of eigenstates in tight-binding chains with power-law potentials. A tight-binding lattice Hamiltonian with a simple power potential with exponent $\mu$ takes the form $$\begin{aligned}
  \mathcal{H}= \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} \left[-t (\ketbra{n}{n+1} + \ketbra{n}{n-1}) + \mathcal{F}n^\mu \ketbra{n}{n}\right]\;.
  \label{eq:poly_ham}
\end{aligned}$$ The eigenvalue equation for an eigenstate at energy $E$ reads as $$\begin{aligned}
  E \psi_{n} = -t \psi_{n + 1} - t \psi_{n - 1} + \mathcal{F}n^\mu \psi_n\;.
  \label{eq:poly_eig}
\end{aligned}$$ The eigenstates localize factorially, i.e. super-exponentially in $n$ for the linear potential $\mu=1$, e.g. the Wannier-Stark case . This implies that the ratio $\psi_n/\psi_{n-1}$ decays asymptotically as $n^{-1}$ for large $n$, e.g. far away from the peak of the eigenstate. For larger exponents ($\mu > 1$), we expect an even stronger decay with $n$, and we use this observation to approximate the eigenvalue equation for very large $n$, away from the peak of the localized eigenstate. Hence we neglect $E \psi_n$ and $t\psi_{n + 1}$ compared to the other terms in Eq. <a href="#eq:poly_eig" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:poly_eig">[eq:poly_eig]</a> for a finite eigenvalue $E$, a finite hopping strength $t$ and a finite field strength $\mathcal{F}$, and simplify the eigenequation to a simple recursion $$\begin{aligned}
  \frac{t}{\mathcal{F}} n^{-\mu} \psi_{n - 1} \approx \psi_n\;.
\end{aligned}$$ This resolves into $$\begin{aligned}
  \label{eq:decay_prob}
  \psi_{n + n_0} \approx {\left(\frac{t}{\mathcal{F}}\right)}^n {(n!)}^{-\mu} \psi_{n_0}\;,
\end{aligned}$$ where $n_0$ is the peak of the eigenstate. Similar arguments apply for very large negative values of $n$, i.e., on other side of the peak of an eigenstate $\psi_n$.

We start by discussing the localization properties of eigenstates in tight-binding chains with power-law potentials. A tight-binding lattice Hamiltonian with a simple power potential with exponent $\mu$ takes the form $$\begin{aligned}
  \mathcal{H}= \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} \left[-t (\ketbra{n}{n+1} + \ketbra{n}{n-1}) + \mathcal{F}n^\mu \ketbra{n}{n}\right]\;.
  \label{eq:poly_ham}
\end{aligned}$$ The eigenvalue equation for an eigenstate at energy $E$ reads as $$\begin{aligned}
  E \psi_{n} = -t \psi_{n + 1} - t \psi_{n - 1} + \mathcal{F}n^\mu \psi_n\;.
  \label{eq:poly_eig}
\end{aligned}$$ The eigenstates localize factorially, i.e. super-exponentially in $n$ for the linear potential $\mu=1$, e.g. the Wannier-Stark case . This implies that the ratio $\psi_n/\psi_{n-1}$ decays asymptotically as $n^{-1}$ for large $n$, e.g. far away from the peak of the eigenstate. For larger exponents ($\mu > 1$), we expect an even stronger decay with $n$, and we use this observation to approximate the eigenvalue equation for very large $n$, away from the peak of the localized eigenstate. Hence we neglect $E \psi_n$ and $t\psi_{n + 1}$ compared to the other terms in Eq. <a href="#eq:poly_eig" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:poly_eig">[eq:poly_eig]</a> for a finite eigenvalue $E$, a finite hopping strength $t$ and a finite field strength $\mathcal{F}$, and simplify the eigenequation to a simple recursion $$\begin{aligned}
  \frac{t}{\mathcal{F}} n^{-\mu} \psi_{n - 1} \approx \psi_n\;.
\end{aligned}$$ This resolves into $$\begin{aligned}
  \label{eq:decay_prob}
  \psi_{n + n_0} \approx {\left(\frac{t}{\mathcal{F}}\right)}^n {(n!)}^{-\mu} \psi_{n_0}\;,
\end{aligned}$$ where $n_0$ is the peak of the eigenstate. Similar arguments apply for very large negative values of $n$, i.e., on other side of the peak of an eigenstate $\psi_n$.

It follows from the normalization condition of the probability distribution that $\psi_{n_0}$ is a finite constant. Therefore the eigenstate decays super-exponentially for large positive lattice sites $n$ as $$\begin{aligned}
  {\left(\frac{t}{\mathcal{F}}\right)}^n {(n!)}^{-\mu} \approx {\left(\frac{t n^{-\mu}}{\mathcal{F}e^{-\mu}}\right)}^n {(2\pi n)}^{-\frac{\mu}{2}} \approx e^{-\mu n \ln(n)}\;.
\end{aligned}$$ Here the Stirling’s approximation $n! \approx \sqrt{2\pi n}{(n/e)}^n$ for large $n$ was used. Therefore we expect the same super-exponential decay of the eigenstates for any power-law potential $n^\mu$, up to the factor $\mu$ in the exponent.

We note that this asymptotic behavior is very different from the asymptotic localization properties in the continuous 1D space $\{x \in \mathbb{R}\}$ where the eigenvalue equation is $$\begin{aligned}
  E \psi_x = - \frac{d^2\psi_x}{dx^2} + \mathcal{F}(x-x_0)^\mu \psi_x\;.
\end{aligned}$$ For the linear case $\mu = 1$ the wave function $\psi_x$ takes the form of Airy function which for $x \gg x_0$ decays as $\left(\mathcal{F}^{1/3} x\right)^{-1/4} e^{-\frac{2}{3} \left(\mathcal{F}^{1/3} x\right)^\frac{3}{2}}$. On the other hand for the simple harmonic potential $\mu = 2$, the wave function $\psi_x$ is a product of a Gaussian and Hermite polynomials, their asymptotic decay is controlled by the Gaussian: $e^{-\frac{\sqrt{\mathcal{F}} x^2}{2}}$. In both cases the asymptotic decay is faster than that of the discrete cases, $e^{-\mu x \ln(x)}$.

We refer to $\abs{\psi_n}^2$ (modulus squared of an eigenstate’s amplitude) as probability at a site $n$ and talk about probability profile $\{\abs{\psi_n}^2\}$ of an eigenstate. As well we refer to the core of a localized eigenstate as its peak. We set the hopping strength $t=1$ and measure $\alpha$ in units of $2\pi \varphi$ with the golden ratio $\varphi=(1+\sqrt{5})/2\approx 1.61803$. We consider finite chains with open boundary condition, numerically diagonalize the Aubry-André Hamiltonian <a href="#eq:full_aa_ham" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:full_aa_ham">[eq:full_aa_ham]</a> with $\phi = \pi/2$ for different winding lengths $\mathcal{W}$ and check the decay of probability profiles of the eigenstates.

To quantify the decay of the probability profiles and discriminate between their exponential and super-exponential decay, we use two metrics. First, we define the local gradient of the logarithms of the probability with respect to the lattice position $$\begin{aligned}
  \Delta(n) = \frac{\ln\abs{\psi_{n+1}}^2 - \ln\abs{\psi_{n-1}}^2}{2} = \ln\abs{\frac{\psi_{n+1}}{\psi_{n-1}}} \;.
  \label{eq:grad_log_prob}
\end{aligned}$$ We refer to it as simply the *gradient* in the text below. A constant in $n$ value of the gradient indicates exponential localization while the decay with $n$ signals faster than exponential decay. For an exponential decay $e^{-2|n|/\xi}$ with a constant localization length $\xi$, $\Delta(n)$ is a step function, with values $\frac{2}{\xi}$ to the left and $-\frac{2}{\xi}$ to right of the probability peak $n=0$. Second, for a super-exponential e.g. factorial decay of the eigenstates of the Wannier-Stark ladder the following ratio takes an asymptotically constant value: $$\begin{aligned}
  R(\mu, n) = \frac{\ln\abs{\psi_{|n| + n_0}}^2 - \ln\abs{\psi_{n_0}}^2}{\mu \ln(|n|!) - |n| \ln|t/\mathcal{F}|} \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} - 2
  \label{eq:ratio_log_prob}
\end{aligned}$$ as follows from Eq. <a href="#eq:decay_prob" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:decay_prob">[eq:decay_prob]</a>. We use the gradient $\Delta$ and the $R$ value in our numerical simulations to detect and quantify the super-exponential decay.

In what follows we refer to *localization on intermediate scales* as simply *localization*, while the usual AA exponential localization is referred to as *asymptotic localization*. In all the other cases the type of localization should be clear from the context.

## Insulating regime: $\abs{\lambda} > 2\abs{t}$

We remind that our main focus $\mathcal{W}\gg 1$. Deep in the insulating regime $\abs{\lambda} \gg 2\abs{t}$ a typical eigenstate with energy close to zero will be located close to a zero of the AA potential and have a span (core) $$\begin{gathered}
  \label{eq:span}
  \mathcal{S}\approx \frac{4 \abs{t}}{\abs{\lambda} \alpha} \approx \frac{2\abs{t}}{\pi \abs{\lambda}} \mathcal{W}, 
\end{gathered}$$ so that $\mathcal{S}\ll \mathcal{W}$ in accord with Eq. <a href="#eq:sin_poly1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:sin_poly1">[eq:sin_poly1]</a>. There are three lengthscales present in the problem: the localization length $\xi$, the winding length $\mathcal{W}$ and the span of an eigenstate $\mathcal{S}$. The span $\mathcal{S}$ is the lengthscale over which the change in potential is of the order of the free particle bandwidth or maximum change in kinetic energy $4\abs{t}$, so that the Hamiltonian appears as that of a free particle on a chain over this lengthscale. The eigenstate shows no significant decay features over that length scale. Beyond this core the eigenstate will decay super-exponentially until the slow quasiperiodic potential returns to its value at the core center.

We remind that our main focus $\mathcal{W}\gg 1$. Deep in the insulating regime $\abs{\lambda} \gg 2\abs{t}$ a typical eigenstate with energy close to zero will be located close to a zero of the AA potential and have a span (core) $$\begin{gathered}
  \label{eq:span}
  \mathcal{S}\approx \frac{4 \abs{t}}{\abs{\lambda} \alpha} \approx \frac{2\abs{t}}{\pi \abs{\lambda}} \mathcal{W}, 
\end{gathered}$$ so that $\mathcal{S}\ll \mathcal{W}$ in accord with Eq. <a href="#eq:sin_poly1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:sin_poly1">[eq:sin_poly1]</a>. There are three lengthscales present in the problem: the localization length $\xi$, the winding length $\mathcal{W}$ and the span of an eigenstate $\mathcal{S}$. The span $\mathcal{S}$ is the lengthscale over which the change in potential is of the order of the free particle bandwidth or maximum change in kinetic energy $4\abs{t}$, so that the Hamiltonian appears as that of a free particle on a chain over this lengthscale. The eigenstate shows no significant decay features over that length scale. Beyond this core the eigenstate will decay super-exponentially until the slow quasiperiodic potential returns to its value at the core center.

In this regime all eigenstates are asymptotically exponentially localized, however their intermediate behavior is controlled and depends strongly on the value of the winding length $\mathcal{W}$. The parameters of the Hamiltonian <a href="#eq:full_aa_ham" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:full_aa_ham">[eq:full_aa_ham]</a> used below – $\lambda = 100, \phi = \pi/2, t = 1$ – are chosen to approximate the Wannier-Stark Hamiltonian <a href="#eq:sin_poly1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:sin_poly1">[eq:sin_poly1]</a> around lattice position $n_0 = 0$. For this choice of parameters the potential energy function <a href="#eq:full_aa_ham" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:full_aa_ham">[eq:full_aa_ham]</a> turns into $\sin(\alpha n)$.

The emergence of the super-exponential localization on intermediate length scales followed by an asymptotic exponential decay in the AA Hamiltonian <a href="#eq:full_aa_ham" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:full_aa_ham">[eq:full_aa_ham]</a> can also be realized in other settings with quasiperiodic potentials. In particular, we consider a discrete-time unitary map, the linear and non-disordered version of the maps used in Ref. . These maps provide a convenient computational tool for studying long-time dynamics of nonlinear systems . It is therefore important to extend these maps to various settings, e.g. to mimic the effect of a DC field.

We start by defining the unitary map. For that we consider a single particle on an infinite 1D chain with time evolution governed by the brickwork action of local unitary operators and described by the following three steps depicted schematically in Fig. <a href="#fig:uc_draw" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="fig:uc_draw">[fig:uc_draw]</a>: All the sites are split into disjoint pairs of the n.n. sites.  
<u>Step I</u>: Probability amplitudes on every pair of sites are mixed by applying an orthogonal $\mathrm{SO}(2)$ matrix: $$\begin{aligned}
  & a_{2n-1}(T + 1) = \cos\theta \psi_{2n-1}(T) + \sin\theta \psi_{2n}(T), \\
  & a_{2n}(T + 1) = -\sin\theta \psi_{2n-1}(T) + \cos\theta \psi_{2n}(T);
\end{aligned}$$ <u>Step II</u>: make a cyclic shift of all the sites by one lattice spacing and mix the amplitudes of the pairs again by applying the same $\mathrm{SO}(2)$ matrix: $$\begin{aligned}
  & b_{2n}(T + 1) = \cos\theta a_{2n}(T + 1) + \sin\theta a_{2n+1}(T + 1), \\
  & b_{2n+1}(T + 1) = -\sin\theta a_{2n}(T + 1) + \cos\theta a_{2n+1}(T + 1);
\end{aligned}$$ <u>Step III</u>: add local random phases $\varepsilon_n$ to all the amplitudes $$\begin{aligned}
  \label{eq:uc_split}
  \psi_n(T + 1) = e^{i \varepsilon_n} b_n(T + 1)\;.
\end{aligned}$$ Combining all the three steps together produces the final unitary map: $$\begin{aligned}
  \psi_{2n}(T+1) & = e^{i \varepsilon_{2n}} [ \cos^2 \theta \psi_{2n}(T) - \cos\theta \sin\theta \psi_{2n-1}(T) \notag \\
  & +\sin\theta \cos\theta\psi_{2n+1}(T) + \sin^2 \theta \psi_{2n+2}(T)], \notag \\
  \psi_{2n+1}(T+1) & = e^{i \varepsilon_{2n+1}} [\cos^2 \theta \psi_{2n+1}(T) - \sin\theta \cos\theta \psi_{2n}(T) \notag \\
  & +\cos\theta \sin\theta \psi_{2n+2}(T) + \sin^2 \theta \psi_{2n-1}(T)]\;.
  \label{eq:UC_evl}
\end{aligned}$$

We start by defining the unitary map. For that we consider a single particle on an infinite 1D chain with time evolution governed by the brickwork action of local unitary operators and described by the following three steps depicted schematically in Fig. <a href="#fig:uc_draw" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="fig:uc_draw">[fig:uc_draw]</a>: All the sites are split into disjoint pairs of the n.n. sites.  
<u>Step I</u>: Probability amplitudes on every pair of sites are mixed by applying an orthogonal $\mathrm{SO}(2)$ matrix: $$\begin{aligned}
  & a_{2n-1}(T + 1) = \cos\theta \psi_{2n-1}(T) + \sin\theta \psi_{2n}(T), \\
  & a_{2n}(T + 1) = -\sin\theta \psi_{2n-1}(T) + \cos\theta \psi_{2n}(T);
\end{aligned}$$ <u>Step II</u>: make a cyclic shift of all the sites by one lattice spacing and mix the amplitudes of the pairs again by applying the same $\mathrm{SO}(2)$ matrix: $$\begin{aligned}
  & b_{2n}(T + 1) = \cos\theta a_{2n}(T + 1) + \sin\theta a_{2n+1}(T + 1), \\
  & b_{2n+1}(T + 1) = -\sin\theta a_{2n}(T + 1) + \cos\theta a_{2n+1}(T + 1);
\end{aligned}$$ <u>Step III</u>: add local random phases $\varepsilon_n$ to all the amplitudes $$\begin{aligned}
  \label{eq:uc_split}
  \psi_n(T + 1) = e^{i \varepsilon_n} b_n(T + 1)\;.
\end{aligned}$$ Combining all the three steps together produces the final unitary map: $$\begin{aligned}
  \psi_{2n}(T+1) & = e^{i \varepsilon_{2n}} [ \cos^2 \theta \psi_{2n}(T) - \cos\theta \sin\theta \psi_{2n-1}(T) \notag \\
  & +\sin\theta \cos\theta\psi_{2n+1}(T) + \sin^2 \theta \psi_{2n+2}(T)], \notag \\
