Similarly to the AA case, we consider $\abs{\alpha} \ll 1$ so that $\abs{\alpha n} \ll \abs{n}$ for all $n$ on a finite chain, so that $e^{- i \alpha n} \approx 1 - i \alpha n$ is justified. Furthermore to allow nearest neighbor hopping only (as in the AA Hamiltonian) we also require $\theta \ll 1$, so that $\cos \theta \approx 1$ and $\sin \theta \approx \theta$. Then the $\alpha \theta$ and $\theta^2$ terms are negligible compared to $\alpha$ and $\theta$ respectively, and the unitary map <a href="#eq:UC_evl" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:UC_evl">[eq:UC_evl]</a> becomes approximately $$\begin{align}
  %
    \psi_{2n}(T+1) & \approx (1 - i \alpha (2 n))\psi_{2n}(T) \\
    & - \theta (\psi_{2n-1}(T) - \psi_{2n+1}(T)), \notag \\
  %
    \psi_{2n+1}(T+1) & \approx (1 - i \alpha (2n+1))\psi_{2n+1}(T) \\
    & - \theta (\psi_{2n}(T) - \psi_{2n+2}(T))\;. \notag
  \end{align}
  \label{sub:uc_ws}$$ The two equations <a href="#sub:uc_ws" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="sub:uc_ws">[sub:uc_ws]</a> are the same up to the relabelling of the lattice sites, and correspond to the discrete time version of the 1D Wannier-Stark Schrödinger Hamiltonian $$\begin{aligned}
%
  & \mathcal{H}_\mathrm{ap}\ket{\psi(T)} = i\left[ \ket{\psi(T+1)} - \ket{\psi(T)}\right], \notag \\
  \label{eq:um_ws}
  & \mathcal{H}_\mathrm{ap} = i \theta \sum_n \ketbra{n}{n+1} -  \ketbra{n}{n-1} + \alpha \sum_n n \ketbra{n}{n}\;.
\end{aligned}$$ From the above expressions we can define the effective DC field strength $\mathcal{F}= \alpha$ and hopping parameter $t = i\theta$. Based on this mapping of the parameters, we expect that the eigenstates of the exact unitary map <a href="#eq:UC_evl" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:UC_evl">[eq:UC_evl]</a> display super-exponential localization on intermediate length scales for $\abs{\alpha}, \theta \ll 1$, followed by the asymptotical exponential localization, similarly to the Aubry-André case. We also define the winding length $\mathcal{W}= \lceil 2\pi/\alpha\rceil$, that controls the intermediate length scale behaviour just like in the AA model above. In analogy to the AA chain reasoning we predict that the span of the core of an eigenstate is $\mathcal{S}\approx \frac{4\abs{\theta}}{\alpha} \approx \frac{2\abs{\theta}}{\pi}\mathcal{W}$ and super-exponential decay should go uninterrupted over one winding length $\mathcal{W}$.

