# Evaluation of the AAH system in the phase-space

Keeping in mind the conditions for decoupling quantum corrections from nonlinear effects, before proceeding with the evaluation of the Hamiltonian dynamics from Eq. <a href="#HamHarper01" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="HamHarper01">[HamHarper01]</a>, a more convenient perspective of the phase-space dynamics can be achieved if the Hamiltonian system is described in terms of a dimensionless version of $H^{W}(q,\,p)$ from Eq. <a href="#nlh" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="nlh">[nlh]</a>, i.e. by $$\label{dimHH}\mathcal{H}(x,\,k) = \mathcal{K}(k) + \mathcal{V}(x),$$ written in terms of dimensionless variables, $x = \left(m\,\omega\,\hbar^{-1}\right)^{1/2} q$ and $k = \left(m\,\omega\,\hbar\right)^{-1/2}p$. In this case, one has $\mathcal{H} = (\hbar \omega)^{-1} H$, $\mathcal{V}(x) = (\hbar \omega)^{-1} V\left(\left(m\,\omega\,\hbar^{-1}\right)^{-1/2}x\right)$ and $\mathcal{K}(k) = (\hbar \omega)^{-1} K\left(\left(m\,\omega\,\hbar\right)^{1/2}k\right)$, where $m$ is a mass scale parameter and $\omega$ is an arbitrary angular frequency. The Wigner function also cast into the dimensionless form of $\mathcal{W}(x, \, k;\,\omega t) \equiv \hbar\, W(q,\,p;\,t)$, suggests that $\hbar$ is absorbed by $dp\,dq\to \hbar\, dx\,dk$ integrations, with Wigner currents then written as $\mathcal{J}_x(x, \, k;\,\omega t)$ and $\mathcal{J}_k(x, \, k;\,\omega t)$, so as to have, $\omega\, \partial_x\mathcal{J}_x \equiv \hbar\, \partial_q J_q(q,\,p;\,t)$ and $\omega \,\partial_k\mathcal{J}_k\equiv \hbar \,\partial_p J_p(q,\,p;\,t)$, which finally can all be recast in the form of $$\begin{aligned}
\label{alexDimW}
\label{imWA}\mathcal{J}_x(x, \, k;\,\tau) &=& +\sum_{\eta=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{i}{2}\right)^{2\eta}\frac{1}{(2\eta+1)!} \, \left[\partial_k^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{K}(k)\right]\,\partial_x^{2\eta}\mathcal{W}(x, \, k;\,\tau),\\
\label{imWB}\mathcal{J}_k(x, \, k;\,\tau) &=& -\sum_{\eta=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{i}{2}\right)^{2\eta}\frac{1}{(2\eta+1)!} \, \left[\partial_x^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{V}(x)\right]\,\partial_k^{2\eta}\mathcal{W}(x, \, k;\,\tau),
\end{aligned}$$ with $$\begin{aligned}
\label{alexDimW}
\mathcal{W}(x, \, k;\,\tau) &=& \pi^{-1} \int^{+\infty}_{-\infty} \hspace{-.35cm}dy\,\exp{\left(2\, i \, k \,y\right)}\,\phi(x - y;\,\tau)\,\phi^{\ast}(x + y;\,\tau),
\end{aligned}$$ where $y = \left(m\,\omega\,\hbar^{-1}\right)^{1/2} s$, $\tau = \omega t$, such that the dimensionless continuity equation is written in terms of the re-defined phase-space coordinates, $\mbox{\boldmath $\xi$} = (x,\,k)$, as $${\partial_{\tau} \mathcal{W}} + {\partial_x \mathcal{J}_x}+{\partial_k \mathcal{J}_k} = {\partial_{\tau} \mathcal{W}} + \mbox{\boldmath $\nabla$}_{\xi}\cdot\mbox{\boldmath $\mathcal{J}$} =0.$$

In the above formulation, since the displacement of quantum numbers exhibited by $\psi_{n\pm1}$ in (<a href="#HamHarper00" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="HamHarper00">[HamHarper00]</a>) corresponds to localized states in adjacent sites, quantum states depicted by $\psi_{n\pm1}\sim \psi({x\pm b}) \equiv \exp[\pm i\,k\,b]\psi(q)$ can be obtained from the translation operation, $\exp[\pm i\,k\,b]$, according to the application of the dimensionless momentum operator, $k$, for a coordinate correspondence given by $(x,\,b)\to(2\pi\beta n, 2\pi\beta)$. The Hamiltonian Eq. (<a href="#HamHarper00" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="HamHarper00">[HamHarper00]</a>) then admits a semi-classical representation reduced to the form of sinusoidal contributions from Eq. <a href="#HamHarper01" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="HamHarper01">[HamHarper01]</a> (i.e. with $w = 0$). Hence, for corresponding coordinate operators, $\hat{x}$ and $\hat{k}$, satisfying $[\hat{x},\hat{k}] = i\,2\pi\beta$, the so-called Peierls phase parameter plays role of an effective Planck constant as it can be set by $2\pi\beta \equiv 1$ in the dimensionless analysis conduced in the previous sections, with $[\hat{x},\hat{k}] = i$.

Furthermore, the phase-space representation of electron dynamics in a two-dimensional crystal is encapsulated by Eq. (<a href="#HamHarper00" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="HamHarper00">[HamHarper00]</a>). To account for nonlinear effects described by the Hamiltonian decomposition $\mathcal{H}(x, k) = \mathcal{K}(k) + \mathcal{V}(x)$, while disregarding the global sign and allowing for a phenomenological variation through an arbitrary parameter $a^2$, the system can be rewritten in the dimensionless form: $$\label{HamHarper01dim}
\mathcal{H}_H(x, k)= \cos(k) +a^2 \cos(x),$$ which admits cyclic analytical solutions in a time-dependent framework. The hypothesis of destroying the periodicity of phase-space trajectories is captured by the $H\to AAH$ Hamiltonian cast into the dimensionless form $$\label{HamHarper01dim}
\mathcal{H}_{_{AAH}}(x,\,k)= w\,k + \cos(k) +a^2(w\, x + \cos(x)),$$ which exhibits (time-dependent) cyclic analytical solutions and therefore work as a feasible departure platform for identifying the implications related to the classicality and quantumness, namely, the Wigner flow deviations from stationary and Liouvillian regimes, according to the formalism introduced in the previous sections. The classical properties of the AAH Hamiltonian <a href="#HamHarper01dim" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="HamHarper01dim">[HamHarper01dim]</a> are depicted in Fig. <a href="#HarperHarper" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="HarperHarper">[HarperHarper]</a> where the phase-space trajectories associated to the corresponding lattice designs are identified for several values of the classical energy, $\mathcal{H}_{_{AAH}} \to \epsilon$, and for varying phenomenological parameters, $a$ and $w$. In particular, one has $w=0$ (first row), $w=0.1$ (second row) and $w=0.4$ (third row), which have been chosen for comparative reasons, from which the role of $w$ in destroying the periodicity of the classical system is evinced.

# Appendix I – Evaluation of Gaussian ensembles

For a Gaussian distribution written as $$\mathcal{G}_\alpha(x,\,k) = \hbar \,G_\alpha(q,\,p) = \frac{\alpha^2}{\pi}\, \exp\left[-\alpha^2\left(x^2+ k^2\right)\right],$$ the associated Wigner flow contributions assume the form of $$\begin{aligned}
\label{imWA2}\partial_x\mathcal{J}_x(x, \, k;\,\tau) &=& +\sum_{\eta=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{i}{2}\right)^{2\eta}\frac{1}{(2\eta+1)!} \, \left[\partial_k^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{K}(k)\right]\,\partial_x^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}(x, \, k),
\\
\label{imWB2}\partial_k\mathcal{J}_k(x, \, k;\,\tau) &=& -\sum_{\eta=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{i}{2}\right)^{2\eta}\frac{1}{(2\eta+1)!} \, \left[\partial_x^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{V}(x)\right]\,\partial_k^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}(x, \, k),
\end{aligned}$$ once the Hamiltonian Eq. (<a href="#dimHH" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="dimHH">[dimHH]</a>) is considered. From Gaussian relations with Hermite polynomials of order $n$, $\mbox{\sc{h}}_n$, one has $$\partial_\zeta^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}(x, \, k) = (-1)^{2\eta+1}\alpha^{2\eta+1}\,\mbox{\sc{h}}_{2\eta+1} (\alpha \zeta)\, \mathcal{G}_{\alpha}(x, \, k),$$ for $\zeta = x,\, k$, which can be reintroduced into Eqs. <a href="#imWA2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="imWA2">[imWA2]</a> and <a href="#imWB2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="imWB2">[imWB2]</a> as to lead to potentially convergent series expansions. This allows for recasting the Wigner flow expressions in an analytical form, which accounts for the overall quantum distortion contributions, i.e. for $\eta$ from $0$ to $\infty$ into Eqs. (<a href="#imWA2" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="imWA2">[imWA2]</a>)-(<a href="#imWB2" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="imWB2">[imWB2]</a>). In particular, for the quantum systems where $\mathcal{V}$ and $\mathcal{K}$ derivatives can be recast in the form of $$\begin{aligned}
\label{t111}
\partial_x^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{V}(x) &=& \lambda^{2\eta+1}_{(x)} \, \upsilon(x),\\
\label{t222}
\partial_k^{2\eta+1}\mathcal{K}(k) &=& \mu^{2\eta+1}_{(k)} \, \kappa(k),
\end{aligned}$$ with $\lambda$, $\upsilon$, $\mu$, and $\kappa$ being arbitrary auxiliary functions, it can be straightforwardly verified that, once substituted into Eqs. <a href="#imWA2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="imWA2">[imWA2]</a> and <a href="#imWB2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="imWB2">[imWB2]</a>, the above expressions lead to $$\begin{aligned}
\label{imWA3}\partial_x\mathcal{J}_x(x, \, k;\,\tau) &=& (+2i) \kappa(k)\,\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}(x, \, k)\,\sum_{\eta=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{i\,\alpha\,\mu_{(k)}}{2}\right)^{2\eta+1}\frac{1}{(2\eta+1)!} \, \mbox{\sc{h}}_{2\eta+1} (\alpha x),\\
\label{imWB3}\partial_k\mathcal{J}_k(x, \, k;\,\tau) &=& (-2i) \upsilon(x)\,\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}(x, \, k)\sum_{\eta=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{i\,\alpha\, \lambda_{(x)}}{2}\right)^{2\eta+1}\frac{1}{(2\eta+1)!} \, \mbox{\sc{h}}_{2\eta+1} (\alpha k).
\end{aligned}$$ Finally, by noticing that $$\sum_{\eta=0}^{\infty}\mbox{\sc{h}}_{2\eta+1} (\alpha \zeta)\frac{s^{2\eta+1}}{(2\eta+1)!} = \sinh(2s\,\alpha\zeta) \exp[-s^2],$$ one gets Eqs. <a href="#imWA4" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="imWA4">[imWA4]</a> and <a href="#imWB4" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="imWB4">[imWB4]</a>.

