        \bar{T}_0(k)-1 & \bar{T}_1(k) & \cdots & \bar{T}_{N-1}(k)\\
\bar{T}_1(k) & \bar{T}_0(k)-1 & \cdots & \bar{T}_{N-2}(k)\\
\bar{T}_2(k) & \bar{T}_1(k) & \cdots & \bar{T}_{N-3}(k)\\
\vdots&\vdots&\vdots&\vdots\\
\bar{T}_{N-1}(k) & \bar{T}_{N-2}(k) & \cdots & \bar{T}_0(k)-1
    \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}
p_{1}^{(ss)}\\p_{2}^{(ss)}\\\vdots\\p_{N}^{(ss)}
\end{bmatrix}=0
\end{aligned}$$ where $\bar{T}_\Delta(k)=\mathbb{T}_{00\Delta\Delta}(k)$ and $\sum_\Delta \bar{T}_\Delta=1$.

Now, since $\vec{p}^{(ss)}$ is generally a non-uniform vector and changes upon changing the initial condition for a localized system, the only possible solution to this overdetermined set of equations is $\bar{T}=\mathbb{I}$. In practice, we find this condition to hold for $\bar{T}(k\ge k_c)$ for a finite $k_c$, at which $F_m(k\ge k_c)=0$ also holds as discussed in the main text. We also note that for an arbitrary initial condition, it may take more time steps than $k_c$ to reach the steady state. When the system is not localized, the above argument does not hold and we cannot restrict the form of $\bar{T}$ in this way. Numerically, we observe $F_m(k)>0$ for delocalized systems.

