# Some lower bounds for the norm of a Harper operator

We shall consider sequences $(x_n)_n$ and $(y_n)_n$ in $\ell^2$, of the form $$x_n =x_n (\theta)=r^{\vert n\vert}$$ and $$y_n =y_n (\theta )=\begin{cases}
Ar^{\vert k\vert} , & \mbox{\rm if $n=2k$} \\
Br^{\vert k\vert} , & \mbox{\rm if $n=2k+1$} 
\end{cases} ,$$ with $A^2+B^2=1$, and with $0<r=r(\theta)<1$ to be chosen later. We have $$\sum\limits_n x_n^2 =\sum\limits_n y_n^2 =
\sum\limits_n r^{2\vert n\vert} =\frac{1+r^2}{1-r^2} \, ,$$ where we set $\sum\limits_n =\sum\limits_{n\in {\mathbb{Z}}}$.

Using the relations $$\label{4.1}
\sum\limits_k r^{\vert k\vert} \cos (ak+b)
=\frac{(1-r^2)\cos b}{1-2r\cos a+r^2} \, ,$$ $$\label{4.2}
\sum\limits_k r^{\vert k\vert+\vert k-1 \vert} \cos (ak+b)=
\frac{2r(1-r^2)\cos \frac{a}{2} \cos \big( b+\frac{a}{2} \big)}{1
-2r^2 \cos a+r^4} \, ,$$ and $$\sum\limits_k r^{\vert k+1\vert+\vert k-1\vert} =
r^2 -1+\sum\limits_k r^{2\vert k\vert},$$ we find $$\begin{split}
& \| H_\theta \|^2  \geq \frac{1-r^2}{1+r^2} \sum\limits_n 
(2x_n \cos 2n\pi \theta +x_{n+1}+x_{n-1})^2 \\
& =\frac{1-r^2}{1+r^2} \sum\limits_n \big( 2r^{\vert n\vert}
\cos 2n\pi \theta +r^{\vert n+1\vert} +r^{\vert n-1 \vert} \big)^2 \\
& =6-\frac{(1-r^2)^2}{1+r^2} +\frac{2(1-r^2)^2}{1-2r^2 \cos 4\pi \theta
+r^4} +8\, \frac{2r}{1+r^2} \cdot 
\frac{(1-r^2)^2 \cos^2 \pi \theta}{1-2r^2 \cos 2\pi \theta +r^4} \, .
\end{split}$$

Taking $r=\tan (\alpha /2)$ with $0<\alpha<\pi /2$, it follows that $\| H_\theta \|^2$ is greater or equal than $$\sup\limits_{0<\alpha <\pi/2}
\Bigg( 6-\frac{4\cos^2 \alpha}{1+\cos \alpha} +2\cos^2 \alpha
\bigg( \frac{1}{1-\sin^2 \alpha \cos^2 2\pi \theta}
+\frac{4\sin \alpha \cos^2 \pi \theta}{1-\sin^2 \alpha \cos^2 \pi \theta}
\bigg) \Bigg) .$$

Taking $r=\tan (\alpha /2)$ with $0<\alpha<\pi /2$, it follows that $\| H_\theta \|^2$ is greater or equal than $$\sup\limits_{0<\alpha <\pi/2}
\Bigg( 6-\frac{4\cos^2 \alpha}{1+\cos \alpha} +2\cos^2 \alpha
\bigg( \frac{1}{1-\sin^2 \alpha \cos^2 2\pi \theta}
+\frac{4\sin \alpha \cos^2 \pi \theta}{1-\sin^2 \alpha \cos^2 \pi \theta}
\bigg) \Bigg) .$$

Chosing $\alpha$ such that $$\cos^2 \alpha =\frac{\sin \pi \theta}{1+\sin \pi \theta} \, ,$$ we arrive at $$\label{4.3} 
\| H_\theta \|^2 \geq f_1 (\theta )^2 \qquad 
\theta \in [0,1/2] ,$$ where $f_1 (\theta)$ is defined in <a href="#I10" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="I10">[I10]</a>.

In particular, we infer from <a href="#4.3" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.3">[4.3]</a> and <a href="#I10" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="I10">[I10]</a> that $$\label{4.4}
\min\limits_{1/4 \leq \theta \leq 1/2} \| H_\theta \| \geq 
\min\limits_{1/4< \theta < 1/2} f_1 (\theta) \approx
\sqrt{6.59303} \approx 2.56769 .$$

In particular, we infer from <a href="#4.3" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.3">[4.3]</a> and <a href="#I10" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="I10">[I10]</a> that $$\label{4.4}
\min\limits_{1/4 \leq \theta \leq 1/2} \| H_\theta \| \geq 
\min\limits_{1/4< \theta < 1/2} f_1 (\theta) \approx
\sqrt{6.59303} \approx 2.56769 .$$

To get a better estimate in a neighborhood of $1/4$ and $1/2$, we use the sequence $(y_n)_n$ and relations <a href="#4.1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.1">[4.1]</a> and <a href="#4.2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.2">[4.2]</a>, to collect $$\begin{split}
\sum\limits_n & (2y_n \cos 2n\pi \theta +y_{n+1}+y_{n-1} )^2 =
\sum\limits_k \Big( 2Ar^{\vert k\vert} \cos 4k\pi \theta +B
(r^{\vert k\vert}+r^{\vert k-1} )\Big)^2 \\
& \qquad \qquad +\sum\limits_k \Big( 2Br^{\vert k\vert} 
\cos (4k+2)\pi \theta +A (r^{\vert k\vert} +r^{\vert k+1\vert} )\Big)^2 \\
& =4A^2 \sum\limits_k r^{2\vert k\vert} \cos^2 4k\pi \theta +
4B^2 \sum\limits_k \cos^2 (4k+2)\pi \theta \\ & \qquad +2(A^2+B^2)
\sum\limits_k (r^{2\vert k\vert}+r^{\vert k\vert +\vert k-1\vert}) \\
& \qquad +4AB \sum\limits_k r^{2\vert k\vert}
\big(\cos 4k\pi \theta+\cos (4k+2)\pi \theta \big) \\ & \qquad
+4AB \sum\limits_k r^{\vert k\vert+\vert k-1\vert}
\big( \cos 4k\pi \theta +\cos (4k-2)\pi \theta \big) .   
\end{split}$$ This further equals $$\begin{split}
4\sum\limits_k & r^{2\vert k\vert} +2A^2 \sum\limits_k 
r^{2\vert k\vert} \cos 8k\pi \theta +2B^2 \sum\limits_k
r^{2\vert k\vert} \cos (8k+4)\pi \theta \\ & \qquad +2(A^2+B^2)
\sum\limits_k r^{\vert k\vert+\vert k-1\vert} 
+8AB \cos \pi \theta \sum\limits_k 
r^{2\vert k\vert} \cos (4k+1)\pi \theta \\ & \qquad  
+8AB \cos \pi \theta \sum\limits_k r^{\vert k\vert+\vert k-1\vert}
\cos (4k-1)\pi \theta \\
& =4\, \frac{1+r^2}{1-r^2} 
+2A^2 \, \frac{1-r^4}{1-2r^2 \cos 8\pi \theta +r^4}
+2B^2 \, \frac{(1-r^4)\cos 4\pi \theta}{1-2r^2 \cos 8\pi \theta +r^4} \\
& =\frac{4r}{1-r^2} +\frac{8AB \cos^2 \pi \theta (1-r^4)}{
1-2r^2 \cos 4\pi \theta} \\ & \qquad 
+8AB\cos^2 \pi \theta \cos 2\pi \theta\cdot \frac{2r}{1-r^2} \cdot
\frac{1-r^4}{1-2r^2 \cos 4\pi \theta +r^4} \, .
\end{split}$$

To get a better estimate in a neighborhood of $1/4$ and $1/2$, we use the sequence $(y_n)_n$ and relations <a href="#4.1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.1">[4.1]</a> and <a href="#4.2" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.2">[4.2]</a>, to collect $$\begin{split}
\sum\limits_n & (2y_n \cos 2n\pi \theta +y_{n+1}+y_{n-1} )^2 =
\sum\limits_k \Big( 2Ar^{\vert k\vert} \cos 4k\pi \theta +B
(r^{\vert k\vert}+r^{\vert k-1} )\Big)^2 \\
& \qquad \qquad +\sum\limits_k \Big( 2Br^{\vert k\vert} 
\cos (4k+2)\pi \theta +A (r^{\vert k\vert} +r^{\vert k+1\vert} )\Big)^2 \\
& =4A^2 \sum\limits_k r^{2\vert k\vert} \cos^2 4k\pi \theta +
4B^2 \sum\limits_k \cos^2 (4k+2)\pi \theta \\ & \qquad +2(A^2+B^2)
\sum\limits_k (r^{2\vert k\vert}+r^{\vert k\vert +\vert k-1\vert}) \\
& \qquad +4AB \sum\limits_k r^{2\vert k\vert}
\big(\cos 4k\pi \theta+\cos (4k+2)\pi \theta \big) \\ & \qquad
+4AB \sum\limits_k r^{\vert k\vert+\vert k-1\vert}
\big( \cos 4k\pi \theta +\cos (4k-2)\pi \theta \big) .   
\end{split}$$ This further equals $$\begin{split}
4\sum\limits_k & r^{2\vert k\vert} +2A^2 \sum\limits_k 
r^{2\vert k\vert} \cos 8k\pi \theta +2B^2 \sum\limits_k
r^{2\vert k\vert} \cos (8k+4)\pi \theta \\ & \qquad +2(A^2+B^2)
\sum\limits_k r^{\vert k\vert+\vert k-1\vert} 
+8AB \cos \pi \theta \sum\limits_k 
r^{2\vert k\vert} \cos (4k+1)\pi \theta \\ & \qquad  
+8AB \cos \pi \theta \sum\limits_k r^{\vert k\vert+\vert k-1\vert}
\cos (4k-1)\pi \theta \\
& =4\, \frac{1+r^2}{1-r^2} 
+2A^2 \, \frac{1-r^4}{1-2r^2 \cos 8\pi \theta +r^4}
+2B^2 \, \frac{(1-r^4)\cos 4\pi \theta}{1-2r^2 \cos 8\pi \theta +r^4} \\
& =\frac{4r}{1-r^2} +\frac{8AB \cos^2 \pi \theta (1-r^4)}{
1-2r^2 \cos 4\pi \theta} \\ & \qquad 
+8AB\cos^2 \pi \theta \cos 2\pi \theta\cdot \frac{2r}{1-r^2} \cdot
\frac{1-r^4}{1-2r^2 \cos 4\pi \theta +r^4} \, .
\end{split}$$

Hence $$\begin{split}
\| H_\theta \|^2 & \geq 4+\frac{4r}{1+r^2}+
\frac{2(A^2+B^2 \cos 4\pi \theta)(1-r^2)^2}{
1-2r^2 \cos 8\pi \theta +r^4} \\
& \qquad +8AB\cos^2 \pi \theta \bigg( 1+\frac{2r}{1+r^2} \, 
\cos 2\pi \theta \bigg) \frac{(1-r^2)^2}{1-2r^2 \cos 4\pi \theta +r^4} \, .
\end{split}$$

Taking $r=\tan (\alpha /2)$ with $0<\alpha <\pi /2$, we find $$\label{4.5}
\begin{split}
\| H_\theta \|^2 & \geq 4+2\sin \alpha +
\frac{2(A^2+B^2 \cos 4\pi \theta )\cos^2 \alpha}{
1-\sin^2 \alpha \cos^2 4\pi \theta} \\
& \qquad +8AB \cos^2 \pi \theta (1+\sin \alpha \cos 2\pi \theta) \,
\frac{\cos^2 \alpha}{1-\sin^2 \alpha \cos^2 2\pi \theta} \, .
\end{split}$$

Taking also $$\sin^2 \alpha =\frac{1-\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}{\cos^2 4\pi \theta}
=\frac{1}{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert} \, ,
\qquad \cos^2 \alpha =\frac{\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}{
1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert} \, ,$$ we get $$\frac{\cos^2 \alpha}{1-\sin^2 \alpha \cos^2 4\pi \theta} 
=\frac{1}{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}$$ and $$\frac{\cos^2 \alpha}{1-\sin^2 \alpha \cos^2 2\pi \theta} =
\frac{2\vert \cos 2\pi \theta \vert}{2\vert \cos 2\pi \theta \vert +
\vert \sin 2\pi \theta \vert} =\frac{2}{2+\vert \tan 2\pi \theta \vert} \, .$$

Hence <a href="#4.5" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.5">[4.5]</a> gives $$\label{4.6}
\begin{split}
\| H_\theta \|^2 & \geq 4+\frac{2}{\sqrt{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}}
+\frac{2(A^2+B^2 \cos 4\pi \theta)}{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert} \\
& \qquad +\frac{16AB \cos^2 \pi \theta}{2+\vert \tan 2\pi \theta \vert}
\bigg( 1+\frac{\cos 2\pi \theta}{\sqrt{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}}
\bigg) .
\end{split}$$

Hence <a href="#4.5" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.5">[4.5]</a> gives $$\label{4.6}
\begin{split}
\| H_\theta \|^2 & \geq 4+\frac{2}{\sqrt{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}}
+\frac{2(A^2+B^2 \cos 4\pi \theta)}{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert} \\
& \qquad +\frac{16AB \cos^2 \pi \theta}{2+\vert \tan 2\pi \theta \vert}
\bigg( 1+\frac{\cos 2\pi \theta}{\sqrt{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}}
\bigg) .
\end{split}$$

Using also the equality $$\label{4.7}
\max\limits_{A^2+B^2=1} 2(\alpha_0 A^2 +
\beta_0 B^2 +\gamma_0 AB )=\alpha_0+\beta_0
+\sqrt{(\alpha_0 -\beta_0)^2 +\gamma_0^2}  ,$$ we infer from <a href="#4.6" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="4.6">[4.6]</a>, with $$\begin{split}
\alpha_0 & =\frac{1}{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert} \, ,
\qquad \beta_0 =\frac{\cos 4\pi \theta}{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}
\\ \gamma_0  &
=\frac{8\cos^2 \pi \theta}{2+\vert \tan 2\pi \theta \vert}
\bigg( 1+\frac{\cos 2\pi \theta}{\sqrt{1+\vert \sin 4\pi \theta \vert}} 
\bigg) \, ,
\end{split}$$ that $$\label{4.8}
\| H_\theta \|^2 \geq f_2 (\theta)^2 \qquad 0\leq \theta \leq 1/2 ,$$ with $f_2(\theta)$ given by <a href="#I11" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="I11">[I11]</a>.

If we take $$z_n =\begin{cases} \sqrt{1/10} \sin \alpha
& \mbox{ if $n=\pm 2$} \\
\sqrt{2/5} \sin \alpha & \mbox{\rm if $n=\pm 1$} \\
\cos \alpha & \mbox{\rm if $n=0$} \\
0 & \mbox{\rm if $n\neq 0,\pm 1,\pm 2$},
\end{cases}$$ for some $\alpha \in {\mathbb{R}}$, then $\sum\limits_n z_n^2 =1$, and so we get $$\begin{split}
\| H_\theta \|^2 & \geq 2\bigg( \frac{\sin \alpha}{\sqrt{10}} \bigg)^2
+2\bigg( \frac{2\cos 4\pi \theta \sin \alpha}{\sqrt{10}}
+\sqrt{\frac{2}{5}} \sin \alpha \bigg)^2 \\
& =2\bigg( 2\cos 2\pi \theta \sqrt{\frac{2}{5}} \sin \alpha
+\cos \alpha +\frac{\sin \alpha}{\sqrt{10}} \bigg)^2 
+\bigg( 2\cos \alpha +2\sqrt{\frac{2}{5}} \sin \alpha \bigg)^2 .
\end{split}$$

